501:
** ἀντλ-έω**,
(ἄντλος) **bale out bilge-water, bale the ship,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 673, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 19.}}
**2** generally, **draw water,** ἀντλέει καὶ ἐγχέει{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.119, NT.John.2.8, etc.}}; οἷον ἐκ κρήνης ἐπ᾽ ὀχετοὺς ἀ. **draw** as from a well, **and pour into..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 79a; διὰ χώνης τοῖσι βουλομένοις πιεῖν 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.31: proverbial of labour in vain, ἠθμῷ ἀντλεῖν}} **draw water** in a sieve, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1344b25; εἰς τετρημένον πίθον ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.40}}; but ἐκ πίθω ἀ. of one who has abundance in store, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.13; ἕτοιμον ἀ. 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.14.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **drain dry,** i.e.,
**II.1** **use the utmost, make the most of,** τὰν ἔμπρακτον ἄντλει μαχανάν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.62: but more commonly}}
**II.2** of toil, suffering, etc., **drain to the dregs,** τὴν παροῦσαν ἀντλήσω τύχην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 377; τλημόνως ἤντλουν κακά [prev. author] “Ch.” 748; λυπρὸν ἀντλήσει βίον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 898}}; δέκα ἀντλήσας ἔτη variant {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 433.}}
**II.3** **squander,** πατρῴαν κτῆσιν ἀντλεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1291.}}
**III** _passive_, ἀντλούμενος ὄλβῳ **flooded with,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 4.92.}}

504:
** ἄνυδρ-ος**, ον,
(ὕδωρ) **waterless,** of arid countries, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 24, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.185; γῆ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 1; δάπεδα}} Trag. cited in Phot.p.151 R.; especially **without spring-water,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.7 codices, compare 149, 3.5}}; ἡ ἄνυδρος (i.e. γῆ) {{LXX+4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.4 and 9, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 103, LXX.Isa.44.3}}; of seasons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3, 14; θέρος [prev. author] “Aër.” 10; in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1085 (Lyric poetry)}}, of a corpse, **deprived of funeral lustrations; unwatered,** σμύρνα{{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 89 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** ἄνυδρον, τό, ={στρύχνον μανικόν}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.73.}}

505:
** ἀνυπό-κρῐτος**, ον,
**without dissimulation,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Wis.5.18, NT.Rom.12.9, NT.Jam.3.17}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.5.}}
**II** **undramatic,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 194.}}
**III** in punctuation, of a stop in a simple sentence, opposed to ἐνυπόκριτος (which see), {{Refs|Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.24H.}}

506:
** ἀνυπό-τακτος**, ον,
of persons or things, **not made subject,** τινί{{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Heb.2.8, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.6.6, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.1.161; ἀ. ὁ βασιλεύς 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.30}}; **unrestrained, free,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.473, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.10.1.}}
**2** **not to be classified under heads, confused,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.36.4}}; **irregular,** ποιήματα, of dithyrambs, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1014.12, 2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 2.15.}}
**II** of persons, **independent,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 61}}; in bad sense, **unruly,** {{NT|NT.1Tim.1.9, NT.Tit.1.6 and 10, “PMag.Par.” 1.1367}}. _adverb_ **-τως** **impatiently,** [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {ἀστέκτως}.}}]
**III** of Verbs, **having no first aorist,** {{Refs|AB 1087.}}

507:
** ἄνω**,
**B**_Aeolic dialect_ ὄνω, _adverb_, (ἀνά):
**I** with Verbs implying Motion, **upwards,** ἄ. ὤθεσκε ποτὶ λόφον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.596}}; ἄ. ἀπὸ θαλάσσης ἀναπλεῖν **up** stream, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.155; ἄ. ποταμῶν χωροῦσι παγαί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 410 (Lyric poetry), hence “ἄ. ποταμῶν”, proverbial, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.287, etc.; κόνις δ᾽ ἄ. φορεῖτο 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 714; κονιορτὸς ἄ. ἐχώρει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.34}}; ἡ ἄ. ὁδός the **upward** road, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621c}}; ἄ. ἰόντι going **up the country** (i.e. **inland,** see{{Refs 5th c.BC+|below 11.1f), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.8; ἄ. κάτω, see below 11.2}}; πέμπειν ἄ., i.e. from the nether world, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 645 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ch.” 147; σύριγγες ἄ. φυσῶσιμέλαν μένος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1412 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** with Verbsimplying Rest, **aloft, on high,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 240}}, etc.; τὸ ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 248a, etc.}}
**II.b** **on earth,** opposed to **the world below,** νέρθε κἀπὶ γῆς ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 416; ἡνίκ᾽ ἦσθ᾽ ἄ. [prev. author] “El.” 1167; ἄ. βλέπειν [prev. author] “Ph.” 1348; ἄ. ἐπὶ [τῆς] γῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 109c}}; οἱ ἄ. the **living,** opposed to οἱ κάτω the **dead,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1068, compare “Ph.” 1348, etc.}}; τὰ ἄ. πράγματα the world **above,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 1.}}
**II.c** **in heaven,** opposed to **earth,** οἱ ἄ. θεοί the gods **above,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1072; κῆρυξ τῶν ἄ. τε καὶ κάτω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 124}}: especially in **NT,** ἐκ τῶν ἄ. εἰμί{{NT|NT.John.8.23; ἡ ἄ. Ἱερουσαλήμ NT.Gal.4.26; ἡ ἄ. κλῆσις NT.Phil.3.14.}}
**II.d** generally, of relative position, ὁ δῆμος ἄ. καθῆτο **in the upper quarter** of the city, i.e. the Pnyx, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.169}}; ἡ ἄ. βουλή, i.e. the Areopagus, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 19}}; βαλλόμενοι ὑπὸ τῶν ἄ. by those **above on the roofs,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.48; τὰ ἄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.25}}; τὰ ἄ. τῆς οἰκίας, opposed to θεμέλια, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 1.2; οἱ ἄ. τόποι “OGI” 111.17.}}
**II.e** geographically, **on the upper side,** i.e. **on the north,** ἄ. πρὸς βορέην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.72}}; οὔτε τὰ ἄ. χωρία οὔτε τὰ κάτω [οὔτε τὰ πρὸς τὴν ἠῶ οὔτε τὰ πρὸς τὴν ἑσπέρην] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.142; ὁ ἄ. τόπος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 435e.}}
**II.f** **inward from the coast,** ἡ ἄ. Ἀσίη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95; τὰ ἄ. τῆς Ἀσίης [prev. work] 177}}; ἡ ἄ. ὁδός the **upper** or **inland** road, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.128, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.8}}; ἡ ἄ. πόλις, opposed to **the Piraeus,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.48}}; in full, οἱ ἀπὸ θαλάσσης ἄ.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 83; ἡ ἄ. Μακεδονία 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 11}}; ὁ ἄ. βασιλεύς the king **of the upper country,** i.e. of Persia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.28.}}
**II.g** in the race-course, τὰ ἄ. **turning-post,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 613b}}; compare κάτω.
**II.h** in the body, τὰ ἄ. the **upper** parts, opposed to τὸ κάτω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 741b28, +others; ἡ ἄ. κοιλία [prev. author] “Mete.” 360b23.}}
**i** of Time, **formerly, of old,** εἰς τὸ ἄ. reckoning **upwards** or **backwards,** of generations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175b}}; οἱ ἄ. men **of olden time,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Criti.” 110b; οἱ ἄ. τοῦ γένους [prev. author] “Lg.” 878a}}; αἱ ἄ. μητρός the mother's **lineal ancestors,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 461c, compare below c; ἐν τοῖς ἄ. χρόνοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.310.}}
**k** **above,** in referring to a passage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 508e; ἐν τοῖς ἄ. λόγοις “R.” 603d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1412b33, etc.}}
**1** of tones in the voice, οἱ ἄ. τόνοι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 3.}}
**1.m** _metaphorically_, ἄ. βαίνειν walk **proudly,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.13; ἄ. φρονεῖν 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.23.}}
**1.n** **higher, more general,** of κατηγορίαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.82a23.}}
**2** ἄ. καὶ κάτω **up and down, to and fro,** εἷρπ᾽ ἄ. τε καὶ κάτω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 953; ἄ. καὶ κ. φεύγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 21; ἄ. τε καὶ κ. κυκᾶν [prev. author] “Eq.” 866; περιπατεῖν ἄ. κ. [prev. author] “Lys.” 709.}}
**2.b** **upside-down, topsy-turvy,** τὰ μὲν ἄ. κ. θήσω, τὰ δὲ κ. ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.3; πάντ᾽ ἄ. τε καὶ κ. στρέφων τίθησιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 650; τρέπουσα τύρβ᾽ ἄ. κ. [prev. author] “fragment” 311, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 3; ἄ. κ. συγχεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 349; ἄ. καὶ κ. ποιεῖν τὰ πράγματα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.36; τοὺς νόμους στρέφειν 21.19; πόλλ᾽ ἄ., τὰ δ᾽ αὖ κ. κυλίνδοντ᾽ ἐλπίδες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 12.6; πολλάκις ἐμαυτὸν ἄ. κ. μετέβαλλον}}**backwards and forwards,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 96a, compare “Prt.” 356d.}}
**3** ἄ. ἔχειν τὸ πνεῦμα **pant** or **gasp,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 23, cf. Sosicrates Comicus 1.}}
**B** as _preposition_ with _genitive_, **above,** ἡ ἄ. Ἅλυος Ἀσίη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.130, compare 103, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 24; αἱ ἄ. μητρός (see. above 11.1 i)}}; ἄ. τοῦ γόνατος **above** the knee, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.4; ἀπὸ ἄ. τῆς χθονὸς ταύτης LXX.1Ki.14.15.}}
**B.2** with partitive _genitive_, αἰθέρος ἄ. ἑλεῖν uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1092, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1542; γῆς ἥκοντ᾽ ἄ. [prev. author] “HF” 616; μικρὸν προαγαγὼν ἄ. τῶν πραγμάτων 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.34.}}
**C** _comparative_ ἀνωτέρω, _absolutely_, **higher,** ἀ. θακῶν.. Ζεύς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 314}}; ἀ. οὐδὲν τῶν πρηγμάτων προκοπτομένων not getting on any **farther,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.190; ἀδελφῷ ἢ πατρὶ ἢ ἔτι ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 880b; οὐ προήϊσαν ἀ. τὸ πρὸς ἑσπέρης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.130.}}
**C.2** with _genitive_, ἀ. Σάμου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 132; ἀ. γίγνεσθαί τινων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.25}}; ἀ. τῶν μαστῶν **above** them, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.4.17}}; later ἀνώτερον{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.7.2, etc.}}; compare ἀνώτερος.
**C.II** _superlative_ ἀνωτάτω, ἐς τοὺς ἀ. (i.e. στάντας) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.23; ἡ ἀ. κώμη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.11; ἀνῳκίσανθ᾽ ὅπως ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 207}}; ἡ ἀ. ἄσκησις the **highest,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.24.84, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.33, +others; τὰ ἀ. τῶν γενῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 998b18, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.51, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.138; τὰ ἀ. τρία 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.321}}; ἡ ἀ. διαίρεσις Ps.-{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Aphr. “in SE” 20.27.}}

508:
Included with: ** ἀνώγαιον**
or ἀνώγεον, τό, (ἄνω, γαῖα) **anything raised from the ground: the upper floor of a house,** used as a granary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.29 (assuming variant), 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 312}}; as a dining-room, {{NT|NT.Mark.14.15, NT.Luke.22.12.}}
**2** **prison,** {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus (ἀνώγεον in “GDI” 1581 (from Dodona)}}; ἀνάγαιον and ἀνόκαιον are also found in codices,{{Refs|compare “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 405, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus)}}

509:
** ἄνωθεν**
and ἄνωθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 698)}}, _Doric dialect_ ἄνωθα {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.17}}: (ἄνω):—_adverb_ of Place, **from above, from on high,** θεοὺς ἄ. γῆς ἐποπτεύειν ἄχη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1579; ὕδατος ἄ. γενομένου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.75; βάλλειν ἄ. [prev. author] 7.84}}; **from the interior** of a country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.59, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.2}}; especially from inner Asia, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 14}}; from the north, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.105.}}
**2** like{ἄνω}, **above, on high,** opposed to κάτωθεν or κάτω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 871 (uncertain)}}: of the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Supp.” 597 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 717b}}; of men on earth, οἱ ἄ. the **living,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 834 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1014}}; those **on deck** (in a ship), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.63}}; of birds **of the air,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1058 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἡ ἄ. Φρυγία **upper** Phrygia, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.155.}}
**2.b** rarely with _genitive_, ἄ. τοῦ στρατοπέδου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75; τοῦ καρποῦ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 80; τῆς νεώς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 12.}}
**II** in narrative or in quiry, **from the beginning, from farther back,** ἄ. ἄρχεσθαι, ἐπιχειρεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 44d, “Lg.” 781d; ἄ. ἐξετάζειν τὸ γένος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.69, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 23}}; in quotations, **above, earlier,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 249}}, etc.: οἱ ἔμπροσθεν καὶ ἄ. γονεῖς **ancestors,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 18d}}; Κορίνθιαι εἰμὲς ἄ. **by descent,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.91, compare 22.164, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.32}}; πονηρὸς ἄ. a **born** rogue, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.80; ἐκ προγόνων ἄ. τετιμημένος “IG” 22.1072; ἄ. ἀναμάρτητον}}**from early life,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Sto.Herc.” 339.17.16; ἐν τοῖς ἄ. χρόνοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.41.}}
**II.2** τὰ ἄ. **higher, more universal principles,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.97a33.}}
**II.3** **over again, anew, afresh,** φιλίαν ἄ. ποιεῖται[{{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.18.3, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.14, cf. NT.John.3.3; πάλιν ἄ. NT.Gal.4.9, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus see {ἀνάδικοι κρίσει; κτίστης ἄνωθε γενόμενος} “IG” 7.2712.58.}}]

510:
** ἀνωτερικός**, ή, όν,
**upper** in point of place, **inland** (see. ἄνω (B) {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.1f), NT.Act.19.1.}}
**2** of a medicine, **given by the mouth,** τροχίσκος{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.42, compare 4th c.AD: Cassius Felix Medicus 48.}}
**II** τὸ -κόν **medicine which takes effect upwards, emetic,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 29, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.969.}}

512:
** ἀνωφελ-ής**, ές,
**unprofitable, useless,** ἁβροσύναι{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 3.1; γόοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 33; σκιά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1159; πάντα ἀ. ἦν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47; ἀ. αὑτῷ τε καὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 496d, +others}}
**2** **hurtful, prejudicial,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.33; τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 334a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.27}}: _comparative_ **-έστερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 284e}}. _adverb_ **-λῶς**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1095a5, “PLond.” 3.908.28 (2nd c.AD).}}

513:
** ἀξῑν-η**, ἡ,
**axe-head,** ἀξίνην ἐΰχαλκον ἐλαΐνῳ ἀμφὶ πελέκκῳ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.612.}}
**2** **battle-axe** (explanation as δίστομος πέλεκυς by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus), [prev. work] 15.711, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.64.}}
**3** **axe** for hewing wood, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.12, NT.Matt.3.10, NT.Luke.3.9}}. (Cf. Gothic _aqizi,_ O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **æx,** Latin _ascia_ (from ***acsia**).)

514:
** ἄξιος**, ία, ιον
(ος, ον {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 8.314), for Αγ-τιος}} **counterbalancing,**{{Refs|compare ἄγω v1}}: hence properly **weighing as much, of like value, worth as much as,** with _genitive_, βοὸς ἄ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.885}}; νῦν δ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἑνὸς ἄξιοί εἰμεν Ἕκτορος we are not—all together—**worth** one Hector,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.234, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 7.21; πάντων Ζεὺς ἄξιον ἦμαρ ἔδωκεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.719}}; so πολλοῦ ἄ. **worth** much, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 185b, etc.; πλείονος ἄ. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 235b, etc.; πλείστου ἄ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 464d, etc.}}; παντός, τοῦ παντὸς ἄ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 275, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 216c}}; παντὸς ἄ., with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 797}}; λόγου ἄ., ={ἀξιόλογος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.73, etc.}}; σπουδῆς, μνήμης ἄ., {{Refs 6th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.35a,172e:—opposed to to these are οὐδενὸς ἄ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 456; ἢ παντὸς ἢ τὸ παράπαν οὐδενός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 64d; ὀλίγου [prev. author] “Grg.” 497b, etc.; σμικροῦ [prev. author] “R.” 504d, etc.; βραχέος [prev. author] “Lg.” 692c}}; μείονος, ἐλάττονος ἄ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.50, “Cyr.” 2.2.14; πολλαπλασίου τιμήματος ἄ. κτήσεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1306b12}}; also εἰς ὀγδοήκοντα μνᾶς ἄ. **worth** up to a sum of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.10.}}
**2** with _dative_ _person_, σοὶ δ᾽ ἄξιόν ἐστιν ἀμοιβῆς 'tis **worth** a return to thee, i.e. will bring thee a return, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.318; πολέος δέ οἱ ἄξιος ἔσται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.562; βασιλεῖ ἂν πολλοῦ ἄξιοι γένοιντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.14.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **worthy, goodly,** ἄξια δῶρα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.261}}; ἄ. ὦνος a **goodly** price, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.429}}; ὅθεν κέ τοι ἄξιον ἄλφοι it would bring thee **a good price,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|20.383; φέροντες ὅ τι ἕκαστος ἄξιον εἶχε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.2.}}
**3.b** in _Attic dialect_ in an exactly opposite sense, 'good value for the money', i.e. **cheap,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 672, 895}}: _comparative_,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 645; ὡς ἀξιώτατον πρίασθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.18; ὡς ἄ. γεγόνασιν οἱ πυροὶ ἐν τῇ ἀγορᾷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 3.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.6.}}
**4** **deserved, meet, due,** δίκη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 298, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 12.19; χάρις [prev. author] “HG” 1.6.11}}; ἄξια δράσας ἄξια πάσχων **fit** suffering for **fit** deeds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1527, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 735.}}
**5** of persons, οἱ ἑωυτοῦ ἄξιοι those **of** one's own **rank,** his **peers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.107.}}
**6** **sufficient for,** with _genitive_, ἄ. τοῦ πολέμου τὰ χρήματα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.27.}}
**7** αἰδοῦς ἀξίαν.. τὴν προθυμίαν μᾶλλον ἢ θράσους more **like** modesty than rashness, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 291b25.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, in moral relation, **worthy, estimable,** of persons and things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.224, etc.}}; οὐδὲν ἀξία nothing **worth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 445; ἀξίαν κἀπ᾽ ἀξίων [prev. author] “Eu.” 435; ἀξίων γεννητόρων ἤθη φυλάσσεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 735.}}
**II.2** **worthy of, deserving,** mostly with _genitive_ _of things_, ἄξιον φυγῆς, ἄξια στεναγμάτων, γέλωτος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Med.” 1124, “Or.” 1326, “Heracl.” 507; ἐγκωμίων τί ἀξιώτερον ἤ..; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 10.3: with _genitive_ _person_, ποιεῖν ἄξια οὔτε ὑμῶν οὔτε πατέρων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71; ἄξιον τοῦ πατρός 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.80; ἄξια τοῦ Μαραθῶνος διανοεῖσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 5.}}
**II.2.b** with _genitive_ _of things_ et _dative_ _person_, ἡμῖν δ᾽ Ἀχιλλεὺς ἄξιος τιμῆς **is worthy of** honour **at** our **hands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 309; πολλῶν ἀγαθῶν ἄ. ὑμῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 633; ἄ. πλείστου Λακεδαιμονίοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.81; θανάτου τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.1, compare 1.2.62; εἰμὶ δ᾽ οὐ τούτων ὑμῖν ἄ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.217; χάριτος ἄ. τῇ πόλει 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.10; later τιμῆς ἄ. παρὰ πάντων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 3.}}
**II.3** with _infinitive_, Προθοήνορος ἀντὶ πεφάσθαι ἄ. **worthy** to be killed instead of him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.472, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76; τίεσθαι δ᾽ ἀξιώτατος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 531; ἄ. θρήνων τυχεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 924}}; ἄξιοι δουλεύειν **only fit** to be slaves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1254b36; also ἄ. σέβειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 315 (Elmsl.).}}
**II.3.b** ἄξιός εἰμι, like{δίκαιός εἰμι}, I **deserve** to.., ἄξιός εἰμι πληγὰς λαβεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 324; ἄξιός εἰμι ἀπολαῦσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.19}}: _absolutely_, the _infinitive_ being supplied, **authorized** to act, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.132}}; ἄ. γάρ, emphatically, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 143e.}}
**II.3.c** later ἄ. ἵνα{{NT|NT.John.1.27.}}
**II.4** ἄξιόν [ἐστι] 'tis **meet, fit, due,** ἄξιον εἶναι τρεῖς ἑνὸς ἀντὶ πεφάσθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.446; ἄ. μνήμην ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14}}: later with _future_ _infinitive_, ἄ. διαπορήσειν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Didymus Grammaticus}}**in D.**{{Refs|9.15.}}
**II.4.b** with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, τῇ πόλει γὰρ ἄξιον ξυλλαβεῖν τὸν νο̄ρα 'tis **meet** for the city, is **worth** her **while..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 205; τί σοι ζῆν ἄξιο; [prev. author] “Nu.” 1074, compare “Av.” 548; ἄξιόν γε πᾶσιν ἐπολολύξαι [prev. author] “Eq.” 616; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus as ὡς οὐκ ἄξιον εἴη βασιλεῖ ἀφεῖναι κτλ.}} that it was not **meet** for him.., {{Refs|“An.” 2.3.25.}}
**II.4.c** the _infinitive_ is sometimes omitted, ἄξιον γὰρ Ἑλλάδι 'tis **meet** in the eyes of Hellas [so to do], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 8}}; and sometimes the _dative_, ἄξιόν ἐστι **operae pretium est,** it is **worth while,** ἐνθυμηθῆναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.21; γαμεῖν οὐκ ἄξιον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 628.}}
**III** _adverb_ ἀξίως, with _genitive_, ἐμάχοντο ἀξίως λόγου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.112; οὔτε ἑωυτοῦ ἀ. [prev. author] 3.125; οὐκ ἀ. ἀπηγήσιος [prev. passage]; τῆς ἀσικίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39}}; ἀ. τοῦ θεοῦ, τῆς θεᾶς, {{NT+5th c.BC+|“OGI” 331.9 (Pergam.), “Inscription Magn.” 33.30, cf. NT.1Thes.2.12: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 133, etc.}}; κολάσετε ἀξίως **as** they **deserve,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40.}}

515:
** ἀξιόω**,
_perfect_ ἠξίωκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.24}}:—_middle_, see below 111.2:—_passive_, _future_ ἀξιωθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.6, but also ἀξιώσεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 637:}}_aorist_ ἠξιώθην: _perfect_ ἠξίωμαι: (ἄξιος):—**think, deem worthy,**
**I** with _accusative_ et _genitive_, whether in good sense, **think worthy of a reward,** ἡμᾶς ἀξιοῖλόγου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 962; ἑαυτὸν τῶν καλλίστων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.7}}; or in bad, **of a punishment,** γοργύρης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.145}}; ἀ. τινὰ ἀτιμίας Philipp. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.166; κακοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38a}}:—_passive_, ἀξιεύμενος θυγατρὸς τῆς σῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.111}}; λέχη.. τυράννων ἠξιωμένα **deemed worthy of** kings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 366; ἀξιοῦσθαι κακῶν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.10; τοῦ αὑτοῦ ὀνόματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 103e, +others}}
**I.2** with _accusative_ only, **esteem, honour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1114, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 918}}; ἀ. τινὰ προσφθέγμασιν **honour** one with words, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 903}}; of things, **value,** οὐκ ἐξἴσου πάσας ἀξιοῦμεν ὑπολήψεις{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.12}}:—_passive_, καλοῖς ὑμεναίοις ἀξιοῦσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1210: _absolutely_, τύμβον ἀξιούμενον ὁρᾶσθαι [prev. author] “Hec.” 319, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.16.}}
**I.3** **value at** a certain price, ὁπόσης ἂν τιμῆς ἀξιώσῃ τὸ πωλούμενον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 917d.}}
**II** c._accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **think** one **worthy** to do or be, σέ τοι ἠξίωσε ναίειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 572}}; οὐκ ἀξιῶ ’γὼ ’μαυτὸν ἰσχύειν μέγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 182; τί σαυτὸν ἀποτίνειν ἀξιοῖ; 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 93}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.39, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 242}}; διδάσκαλος ἀξιοῦσθαι **to be esteemed as** a teacher, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161d.}}
**II.2** **think fit, expect, require that..,** ἀ. τινὰ ἰέναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.162; ἀ. τινὰ ἀληθῆ λέγειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.4}}; οὐκ ἀ. [ὑμᾶς] τὰ μὴ δεινὰ ἐν ὀρρωδίᾳ ἔχειν we **expect** that you do not.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89, compare 3.44; ἀ. σωτηρίαν ἐμοὶ γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.143}}; ἀ. καὶ παρακαλεῖν τινα with _infinitive_, Canon Laws texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.165; ἀ. ἵνα.. “Inscription Prien.” 53.58, +others}}; simply, **ask, request,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Elephantine Papyri 19.18, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra 1 “Praef.” (_passive_)}}; especially **pray,** τὸν θεὸν ὅπως.. Aristeas 245,{{LXX|cf. LXX.Jer.7.16, “SIG” 1181.1 Aristeas 18}}; also, **ask, inquire** of an oracle, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 1.3.}}
**III** with _infinitive_ only, ἀ. κομίζεσθαι, τυγχάνειν **think one has a right to** receive, **expect** to receive, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.43, 7.15; προῖκα θεωρεῖν ἀ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 6.4; ἄλλο τι ἀξιοῖς ἢ ἀποθανεῖ; 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.5: with a _negative_, οὐκ ἀξιῶ ὑποπτεύεσθαι}} I **think I do** not **deserve** to be suspected, **have a right** not to be.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86:}} —_passive_, ὥστε ἀξιοῦσθαι λῃτουργεῖν so as **to be required** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.64; υἱῷ προθύμως τἀξιούμενον ποιῶν}}**one's duty,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 663.}}
**III.2** **think fit, expect, consent, resolve,** etc., and so in various senses, ἀξιῶ θανεῖν I **consent** to die, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 944, etc.}}; **dare,** ἀξιῶσαι μάχην συνάψαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 335}}; **deign** to do, εἴ τις ἀξιοῖ μαθεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1661, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1413}}; ἀξιῶ χρήματα λαμβάνειν I **do not hesitate** to receive, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 364d, etc.}}; οἶμαι πάντας.. φέρειν ἀξιοῦν ἔρανον I think that all **should be glad** to bring, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.101:}} —frequently with _negative_, οὐδ᾽ ἀξιῶ μνησθῆναι I **do** not **think** them **worth** mentioning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.20; οὐκ ἠξίωσαν οὐδὲ προσβλέψαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 217; οὐκ ἀξιώσαντες.. τοῦτο παθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.102 (but ἀξιοῦτε μὴ ἀντιδοῦναι δίκην 3.66); πείθεσθαι οὐκ ἀξιοῦντες}}**refusing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.4:}} —also in _middle_ (not in _Attic dialect_ Prose), ἀξιοῦσθαι μέλειν **deign** to care for, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 370}}; φονεὺς γὰρ εἶναι ἠξιώσατο **thought fit** to be, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 425}}; οὐκ ἀξιεύμεναι ἀναμίσγεσθαι τῇσι ἄλλῃσι not **condescending** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199}}; οὐκ ἀξιεύμενος ἐς τὸν.. θρόνον ἵζεσθαι not **deeming oneself worthy** to.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.16.}}
**III.3** **think, deem,** ἀξιοῦντες ἀδικέεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.87, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 579, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1343}}; ἑκάτεροι νικᾶν ἠξίουν **claimed** the victory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.55.}}
**IV** **make a claim,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.58; πάντες καθ᾽ ὑπεροχὴν -οῠσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1288a23; ἀξίωσιν ἀ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.7.7}}; ἀξιοῦν τινά τι **make a claim on** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.12.}}
**IV.2** ἐγὼ μὲν οὑτωσὶ περὶ τῆς τύχης ἀξιῶ **hold** this **opinion..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.255}}; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐκ ἀξιῶ I **think** not, {{Refs|[prev. author] 20.12}}: in philosophic language, **lay down, maintain** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (ἀξίωμα 11.2), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 37a10, compare 41b10, Polystr. p.24 W.}}; ἐν τῷ τοιῷδε ἀξιοῦντι in such a **state of opinion,** variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.43.}}

516:
Included with: ** ἄξιος**, ία, ιον
(ος, ον {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 8.314), for Αγ-τιος}} **counterbalancing,**{{Refs|compare ἄγω v1}}: hence properly **weighing as much, of like value, worth as much as,** with _genitive_, βοὸς ἄ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.885}}; νῦν δ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἑνὸς ἄξιοί εἰμεν Ἕκτορος we are not—all together—**worth** one Hector,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.234, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 7.21; πάντων Ζεὺς ἄξιον ἦμαρ ἔδωκεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.719}}; so πολλοῦ ἄ. **worth** much, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 185b, etc.; πλείονος ἄ. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 235b, etc.; πλείστου ἄ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 464d, etc.}}; παντός, τοῦ παντὸς ἄ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 275, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 216c}}; παντὸς ἄ., with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 797}}; λόγου ἄ., ={ἀξιόλογος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.73, etc.}}; σπουδῆς, μνήμης ἄ., {{Refs 6th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.35a,172e:—opposed to to these are οὐδενὸς ἄ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 456; ἢ παντὸς ἢ τὸ παράπαν οὐδενός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 64d; ὀλίγου [prev. author] “Grg.” 497b, etc.; σμικροῦ [prev. author] “R.” 504d, etc.; βραχέος [prev. author] “Lg.” 692c}}; μείονος, ἐλάττονος ἄ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.50, “Cyr.” 2.2.14; πολλαπλασίου τιμήματος ἄ. κτήσεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1306b12}}; also εἰς ὀγδοήκοντα μνᾶς ἄ. **worth** up to a sum of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.10.}}
**2** with _dative_ _person_, σοὶ δ᾽ ἄξιόν ἐστιν ἀμοιβῆς 'tis **worth** a return to thee, i.e. will bring thee a return, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.318; πολέος δέ οἱ ἄξιος ἔσται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.562; βασιλεῖ ἂν πολλοῦ ἄξιοι γένοιντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.14.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **worthy, goodly,** ἄξια δῶρα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.261}}; ἄ. ὦνος a **goodly** price, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.429}}; ὅθεν κέ τοι ἄξιον ἄλφοι it would bring thee **a good price,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|20.383; φέροντες ὅ τι ἕκαστος ἄξιον εἶχε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.2.}}
**3.b** in _Attic dialect_ in an exactly opposite sense, 'good value for the money', i.e. **cheap,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 672, 895}}: _comparative_,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 645; ὡς ἀξιώτατον πρίασθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.18; ὡς ἄ. γεγόνασιν οἱ πυροὶ ἐν τῇ ἀγορᾷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 3.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.6.}}
**4** **deserved, meet, due,** δίκη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 298, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 12.19; χάρις [prev. author] “HG” 1.6.11}}; ἄξια δράσας ἄξια πάσχων **fit** suffering for **fit** deeds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1527, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 735.}}
**5** of persons, οἱ ἑωυτοῦ ἄξιοι those **of** one's own **rank,** his **peers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.107.}}
**6** **sufficient for,** with _genitive_, ἄ. τοῦ πολέμου τὰ χρήματα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.27.}}
**7** αἰδοῦς ἀξίαν.. τὴν προθυμίαν μᾶλλον ἢ θράσους more **like** modesty than rashness, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 291b25.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, in moral relation, **worthy, estimable,** of persons and things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.224, etc.}}; οὐδὲν ἀξία nothing **worth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 445; ἀξίαν κἀπ᾽ ἀξίων [prev. author] “Eu.” 435; ἀξίων γεννητόρων ἤθη φυλάσσεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 735.}}
**II.2** **worthy of, deserving,** mostly with _genitive_ _of things_, ἄξιον φυγῆς, ἄξια στεναγμάτων, γέλωτος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Med.” 1124, “Or.” 1326, “Heracl.” 507; ἐγκωμίων τί ἀξιώτερον ἤ..; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 10.3: with _genitive_ _person_, ποιεῖν ἄξια οὔτε ὑμῶν οὔτε πατέρων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71; ἄξιον τοῦ πατρός 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.80; ἄξια τοῦ Μαραθῶνος διανοεῖσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 5.}}
**II.2.b** with _genitive_ _of things_ et _dative_ _person_, ἡμῖν δ᾽ Ἀχιλλεὺς ἄξιος τιμῆς **is worthy of** honour **at** our **hands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 309; πολλῶν ἀγαθῶν ἄ. ὑμῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 633; ἄ. πλείστου Λακεδαιμονίοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.81; θανάτου τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.1, compare 1.2.62; εἰμὶ δ᾽ οὐ τούτων ὑμῖν ἄ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.217; χάριτος ἄ. τῇ πόλει 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.10; later τιμῆς ἄ. παρὰ πάντων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 3.}}
**II.3** with _infinitive_, Προθοήνορος ἀντὶ πεφάσθαι ἄ. **worthy** to be killed instead of him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.472, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76; τίεσθαι δ᾽ ἀξιώτατος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 531; ἄ. θρήνων τυχεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 924}}; ἄξιοι δουλεύειν **only fit** to be slaves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1254b36; also ἄ. σέβειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 315 (Elmsl.).}}
**II.3.b** ἄξιός εἰμι, like{δίκαιός εἰμι}, I **deserve** to.., ἄξιός εἰμι πληγὰς λαβεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 324; ἄξιός εἰμι ἀπολαῦσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.19}}: _absolutely_, the _infinitive_ being supplied, **authorized** to act, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.132}}; ἄ. γάρ, emphatically, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 143e.}}
**II.3.c** later ἄ. ἵνα{{NT|NT.John.1.27.}}
**II.4** ἄξιόν [ἐστι] 'tis **meet, fit, due,** ἄξιον εἶναι τρεῖς ἑνὸς ἀντὶ πεφάσθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.446; ἄ. μνήμην ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14}}: later with _future_ _infinitive_, ἄ. διαπορήσειν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Didymus Grammaticus}}**in D.**{{Refs|9.15.}}
**II.4.b** with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, τῇ πόλει γὰρ ἄξιον ξυλλαβεῖν τὸν νο̄ρα 'tis **meet** for the city, is **worth** her **while..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 205; τί σοι ζῆν ἄξιο; [prev. author] “Nu.” 1074, compare “Av.” 548; ἄξιόν γε πᾶσιν ἐπολολύξαι [prev. author] “Eq.” 616; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus as ὡς οὐκ ἄξιον εἴη βασιλεῖ ἀφεῖναι κτλ.}} that it was not **meet** for him.., {{Refs|“An.” 2.3.25.}}
**II.4.c** the _infinitive_ is sometimes omitted, ἄξιον γὰρ Ἑλλάδι 'tis **meet** in the eyes of Hellas [so to do], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 8}}; and sometimes the _dative_, ἄξιόν ἐστι **operae pretium est,** it is **worth while,** ἐνθυμηθῆναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.21; γαμεῖν οὐκ ἄξιον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 628.}}
**III** _adverb_ ἀξίως, with _genitive_, ἐμάχοντο ἀξίως λόγου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.112; οὔτε ἑωυτοῦ ἀ. [prev. author] 3.125; οὐκ ἀ. ἀπηγήσιος [prev. passage]; τῆς ἀσικίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39}}; ἀ. τοῦ θεοῦ, τῆς θεᾶς, {{NT+5th c.BC+|“OGI” 331.9 (Pergam.), “Inscription Magn.” 33.30, cf. NT.1Thes.2.12: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 133, etc.}}; κολάσετε ἀξίως **as** they **deserve,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40.}}

517:
** ἀόρᾱ-τος**, ον,
**unseen, invisible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85e, etc.; ἀόρατος ὄψιν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 240.5; τραῦμ᾽ ἀ., ἔρως “APl.” 4.198 (1st c.AD(?): Maecius Epigrammaticus); ἀ. τὸ μέλλον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.29}}; τὸ ἀόρατον **the unseen world, the unseen,** ἐξ οὐρανοῦ καὶ τοῦ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 246a, compare “Tht.” 155e, +others: τὰν ἀ. ἀτραπιτὸν βιότου}}**obscure,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|epigram Gr. 223 (Milet.); ἀ. κατὰ δόξαν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.511d; τὸν ἀ. ὡς ὁρῶν NT.Heb.11.27}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.157, “Placita Philosophorum” 2.24.5.}}
**II** _active_, **not having seen, without experience of,** παντὸς κακοῦ, δεινοῦ, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.21.2, 3.108.6: _absolutely_, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Halc.” 3.}}

518:
** ἀπαγγ-έλλω**,
_future_ -αγγελῶ, _Ionic dialect_ **-έω**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.18}}: _aorist 1_ **ἀπαγγ-ήγγειλα**: _perfect_ **-ήγγελκα**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 16}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ **-ήγγελμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 153c}}: _aorist_ **-ηγγέλθην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.e/, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 672}} later **-ηγγέλην** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 25:}}
**1** of a messenger, **bring tidings, report,** τινί τι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.626, etc., 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.18, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.25, etc.; τι πρός τινα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.22, etc.; ἀ. εἰς τὴν Ἑλλάδα, εἰς τὸ στρατόπεδον, [prev. work] 2.4.4, 6.4.25}}; τὰ παρά τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 2.3.4; ταῦτα περί σου οἴκαδε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 71c, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.122}}; ἀ. ἡδονάς, φόνον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 642, “Andr.” 1241: followed by _relative_ clause, ἐκέλευε τὸν ἄγγελον ἀπαγγέλλειν ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.127, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.5; ἀ. ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.6: _absolutely_, πάλιν ἀ.}}**bring back tidings, report in answer,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.95:}} —_passive_, ἐξ ὧν.. ἀπηγγέλλετό μοι as **he was reported** to me, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.25: with}}_participle_, ἀπηγγέλθη.. ὁ νέκυς ἐκκεκλεμμένος **was reported** to have been stolen away, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.έ.}}
**2** of a speaker or writer, **report, relate,** ὄψις ἀπαγγέλλει{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.210, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1417b9, “Po.” 1448a21, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 20; ὧν ὁ παθὼν ἔνια.. οὐδ᾽ ἂν ἀπαγγεῖλαι δύναιθ᾽ ἑτέρῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.72}}; **describe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.3 (_passive_),compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 16; [Ῥωμαίους] ἡττημένους ὑπὸ τοῦ συγγραφέως ἀπηγγέλθαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.15.11.}}
**3** **recite, declaim,** {{Refs|Chor.in “Rev.Phil.” 1.220.}}
**II** **explain, interpret,** a dream or riddle, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.41.8, LXX.Judg.14.12.}}

520:
** ἀπάγω** [ᾰγ],
**lead away, carry off,** ἀπάγουσι βόας καὶ ἴφια μῆλα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.278; ἀ. τινὰ ἐκτόπιον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1340 (Lyric poetry), compare 1521, etc.}}; προσάγειν.., ἀπάγειν, bring near.., **hold far off,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 336a18}}; ἀ. ἀχλὺν ἀπ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν **remove** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.6.2; τὸ ἱμάτιον ἀπὸ τοῦ τραχήλου 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 12}}; οὐκ ἀπάξετε ταῦτ; **stop** this **fooling!** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.225a}}:—_middle_, **take away for** or **with oneself,** παρθένον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196, compare 4.80, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1105, etc.}}; or **that which is one's own,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.37, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἐς ὀξὺ ἀπηγμένας **brought to a point, tapering off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.64, compare 2.28, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 658b30.}}
**2** **lead away, draw off** troops, τῆς στρατιῆς τὸ πολλόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.164, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.28, +others; ἄπαγε τὸν ἵππον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 32.}}
**2.b** elliptically, **retire, withdraw,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.126, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.34, +others; 'go off', 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Epit.” 3.3.}}
**3** **abduct,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.80, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 16}}:—_passive_, πρὸς ὕβριν -εσθαι{{Refs|[prev. author] “Anach.” 13.}}
**II** **bring back, bring home,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.326; ἀπήγαγεν οἴκαδε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.370, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 941, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.14; ἀ. ὀπίσω 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.117.}}
**III** **return, render what one owes, pay,** τὸν φόρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 707, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.12, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.53}}; **render** service, honour, etc., κώμους πρὸς τάφον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1184; θεωρίαν εἰς Δῆλον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58b.}}
**IV** **arrest and carry off,** ἀπάγετε αὐτὸν παρ᾽ ἐμέ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.114, cf 6.81; δεῖν κἀπάγειν ἐφίετο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 439}}:—_passive_, ἀπαχθέντας παρ᾽ ἑωυτόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.119.}}
**IV.2** law-term, **bring before a magistrate and accuse** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ἀπαγωγή 111), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.85}}; ἀσεβείας **for** impiety, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.27; ἀ. ὡς θεσμοθέτας [prev. author] 23.31; ἀ. τοῖς ἕνδεκα [prev. author] 24.113}}; τὴν ἐπὶ θανάτῳ -εσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1397a30cited in 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 1.12.}}
**IV.3** **carry off to prison,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 486a, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 57; εἰς τὸ δεσμωτήριον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.181, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.80, 35.47 (_passive_): _absolutely_, ὡς γόης ἀπαχθῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 80b; ἀπαχθείς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.15.}}
**V** **lead away, divert** from the subject, especially by sophistry, ἀπὸ τοῦ ὄντος ἐπὶ τοὐναντίον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 262b; ἀ. τινὰ ἀπὸ τῆς ὑποθέσεως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.242; ἀ. τὸ ὀργιζόμενον τῆς γνώμης}}**divert..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.59; ἀπὸ δεινῶν ἀ. τὴν γνώμην [prev. work] 65.}}
**V.b** in Logic, **reduce,** εἰς ἀδύνατον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 29b9}}:—_impersonal_ in _passive_, ἀπῆκται ἄρα εἰς..{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 798.11.}}
**V.c** in later Greek, **reduce, drive** an opposing disputant, ἐπὶ ψεῦδος{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.233}}; εἰς ἀντίφασιν, εἰς ἄτοπον, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Analytica Priora commentaria - pars” 21.31, 58.14}}:—_passive_, εἰς ἀδύνατον ἀπαχθῆναι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.7.25, compare 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Analytica Priora commentaria - pars” 129.2.}}
**V.2** **receive,** ἀπ᾽ ὄψεως.. τὰ δοξάζοντα ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 39b.}}
**V.3** **separate,** ἀπάγεται καὶ χωρίζεται{{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 97b.}}
**VI** simply, **carry,** ἐν ἀριστερᾷ τόξον{{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 795a.}}

521:
** ἀπαίδ-ευτος**, ον,
**uneducated,** παιδεύσωμεν τὸν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 493, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175d; πιθανώτεροι οἱ ἀ. τῶν πεπαιδευμένων ἐν τοῖς ὄχλοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1395b27, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 989}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, **uninstructed in..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.55.}}
**2** **boorish, rude,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 510b, etc.; ῥῆμα ἀ. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 269b; ἀ. βίος 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 284; πνεῦμα 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 213.11}}; ἀ. μαρτυρία **clumsy** evidence, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.70; ζητήσεις NT.2Tim.2.23}}: _comparative_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nicochares Comicus 3.}}
**II** _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 559d; ἀ. ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 247, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 267.4, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.36; φληναφᾶσθαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.227S.}}

522:
** ἀπαίρω**
(compare ἀπαείρω), _future_ ἀπᾱρῶ: _aorist 1_ ἀπῆρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 967}}: _perfect_ ἀπῆρκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.100, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.82: _Ionic dialect_}}_imperfect_ ἀπαιρέεσκον, variant {ἀπαίρεσκον}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186}}:—**lift off,** and so, **carry off, take away,** τὰ ξύλα {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; **remove,** τί τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1608; τινὰ Σπάρτης [prev. author] “Hel.” 1671; in “IT” 967}}, perhaps **get rid of,** νικῶν ἀπῆρα φόνια πειρατήρια:—_passive_, ἀπαίρεται τράπεζα{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 17.5; ἀπό τινος NT.Matt.9.15.}}
**II** **lead** or **carry away,** τὰς νέας ἀπὸ Σαλαμῖνος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.57; μελάθρων ἀ. πόδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 774; ἀ. τινὰ ἐκ χθονός [prev. author] “Hel.” 1520.}}
**II.2** elliptically (i.e. ναῦς, στρατόν, etc.), **sail away, march away, depart,** ἀπαίρειν ἀπὸ Σαλαμῖνος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.60, frequently in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, etc.: with _genitive_, ἀπαίρειν χθονός}} **depart from** the land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 131; Σπάρτης ἀπῆρας νηΐ Κρησίαν χθόνα [prev. author] “Tr.” 944: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἀ. πρεσβείαν}} **to set out on** an embassy, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.163.}}

523:
** ἀπαιτ-έω**,
**demand back, demand to have returned,** especially of things forcibly taken or rightfully belonging to one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2; εἰ μὲν βούλεσθε, αἰτῶ, εἰ δὲ μὴ βούλεσθε, ἀπαιτῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.22}}; τὸ μισθάριον γὰρ ἂν ἀπαιτῇς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.34; τὸ τέλος ὁ χρόνος ἀπαιτεῖ “BCH” 7.278 (from Tralles)}}; τὴν ψυχήν{{NT|NT.Luke.12.20}}; ἀ. τινά τι **demand** something **of** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.122, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 963, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 554, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.22; εὐθύνας ἀ. τινά [prev. author] 18.245; also ἀ. ὅπλα τοῦ πατρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 362; χάριν ἀ. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241a, etc.; τι παρά τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 408a18; also ἀ. δίκην ἐξ ἀδίκων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 398; λόγον ἀ. τινὰ περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 599b}}; ἀ. ὑπέρ τινος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 612d; ἀ. ὑποσχέσεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1164a17}}: with _infinitive_, ἀ. τινὰ ποιεῖν τι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 385.}}
**b** **call down on oneself,** ποινάς{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.59a (and so _middle_, [prev. work] 58a).}}
**c** of things, **require,** νοῦσοι -έουσι σικύην{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.10; περίοδος ἀ. μῆνα τρισκαιδέκατον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agis” 16: _absolutely_, ὅταν αἱ χρεῖαι -ῶσιν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 15.1.}}
**2** **inquire,** ἀπαιτήσομεν αὐτὸν τίνες εἰσίν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 12.3.24.}}
**II** _passive_, of things, **to be demanded in payment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.35.}}
**II.2** of persons, **have demanded of one,** ἀπαιτεῖσθαι εὐεργεσίαν{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 17; τὸ τῆς ψυχῆς χρέος LXX.Wis.15.8; ἀποδώσειν ὅταν ἀπαιτῆται “BGU” 1058.33 (1st c.BC)}}; **yield to a request,** οὐκ ἀπαιτούμεσθα, answering to ἀπαιτῶ σκῆπτρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 602.}}

524:
** ἀπαλγ-έω**,
**put away sorrow for,** τὰ ἴδια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61; ἀ. τὸ πένθος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 22; τὸ πάθος 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 16.}}
**II** generally, **to be despondent,** ἀ. ταῖς ἐλπίσιν{{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.40.4; πρὸς ἐλπίδα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 48.37: _absolutely_, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.35.5, NT.Eph.4.19.}}

525:
** ἀπαλλάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἀπαλλάττω**, _future_ **-ξω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.52}}: _perfect_ ἀπήλλᾰχα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.6}}: _aorist_ ἀπήλλαξα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1537, etc.}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ ἀπήλλαγμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 1128, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.49}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπάλλαγμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.144, 167:}}_aorist_ ἀπηλλάχθην, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπαλλ-{{Refs|[prev. author] 2.152, etc.}}; in _Attic dialect_ ἀπηλλάγην [ᾰ] as always in Prose; also in Trag. (for the most part for the sake of meter, compare however {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 422, “El.” 783 (variant), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 592 (variant), “Andr.” 592)}}: _future_ ἀπαλλαχθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 356, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 940; in Prose, ἀπαλλαγήσομαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28, etc.:}} —_middle_, _future_ (in _passive_ sense) ἀπαλλάξομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.122, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 437, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.83, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἀπαηλλάξαντο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 317, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 64.}}
**A**. _active_, **set free, deliver from** a thing, παιδίον δυσμορφίης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.61}}; τινὰ πόνων, κακῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 83, “Pr.” 773; τινὰ ἐκ γόων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 292; ἐκ φόβου καὶ κακῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.59}}: with _accusative_ only, **release,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 596, etc.; κόπος μ᾽ ἀ. [prev. author] “Ph.” 880.}}
**2** **put away from, remove from,** τί τινος, as ἀ. γῆς πρόσωπον, φρενῶν ἔρωτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 27, “Hipp.” 774 (Lyric poetry); σφαγῆς χεῖρα “IT” 994; χρυσὸν χερός “Hec.” 1222}}; ἀ. τινά τινος **take away** or **remove from** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1046; τινὰ ἀπὸ τῆς πολιορκίας 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 43.32.}}
**3** with _accusative_ only, **put away, remove,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1068, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 354d, etc.}}; μύθοις ἔργ᾽ ἀ. κακά **do away** ill by words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 282.26}}; **get rid of** creditors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.122; τοὺς χρήστας 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.28}}; **get rid of** an opponent, by fair means or foul, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.37; ἀ. τοὺς κατηγόρους 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 29.1; τοὺς Πελοποννησίους ἐκ τῆς χώρας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48}}; **dismiss, send away,** τινά{{Refs|[prev. author] 1.90}}; **remove** or **displace** from an office,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 129; ἀ. τοὺς ὑπηρέτας καὶ θεραπευτῆρας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 11}}; also, **make away with, destroy,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.15.2; ἑαυτόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 70}}; **bring to an end,** λόγον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 790.}}
**4** in Law, **give a release, discharge,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.25, compare 37.1; τοὺς δανείσαντας ἀ. 34.22, compare “PTeb.” 315.16 (2nd c.AD)}}; **discharge** a debt, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 59.1, etc.}}:—so in _passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 51.17.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **get off free, escape,** especially with an _adverb_ added, ῥηιδίως, χαλεπῶς ἀ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10, 20, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.5; ὁ στόλος οὕτως ἀ.}}**came off, ended,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.63, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1288, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 786; οὐκ ὡς ἤθελε ἀπήλλαξεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.16; κάκιον ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 491d, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 199; καταγελάστως ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.38; ἀλυσιτελῶς ἀ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 8.11}}; ἀλύπως ἀ. **get along** well, {{Refs|“PPetr.” 3p.58}}: with _participle_ or _adjective_, χαίρων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.69; ἀθῷοι ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 254d, etc.}}: with _genitive_, **depart from,** βίου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 302 (uncertain reading); τοῦ ζῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 367c; so πῶς ἀπήλλαχεν ἐκ τῆς ὁδο; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.6; ἄριστ᾽ ἀπαλλάττεις ἐπὶ τούτου τοῦ κύβου}}**in respect of..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 73.}}
**B**. _passive_ and _middle_, **to be set free** or **released from** a thing, **get rid of** it, ἀπαλλαχθέντας δουλοσύνης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.170; τυράννων [prev. author] 5.78; τῶν παρεόντων κακῶν [prev. author] 2.120; πημονῆς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 471; φόβου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 783; πραγμάτων τε καὶ μαχῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 293; στρατιᾶς [prev. author] “Ach.” 251; Κλέωνος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; τῆς κακουχίας ἐπὶ τὴν αὑτοῦ σκηνήν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.15.6.}}
**B.2** **get off, escape,** mostly with some _adjective_ or _adverb_ added (as in _active_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11), ῥηιδίως ἀ. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 3; ἀγῶνος ἀ. καλῶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 346}}; ἀζήμιος ἀπαλλαγῆναι, ἀπαλλάττεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 271, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 721d.}}
**B.3** _absolutely_, **to be acquitted,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.39.}}
**B.4** of a point under discussion, **to be dismissed as settled,** τοῦτο ἀπήλλακται μὴ.. τὸ φίλον φίλον εἶναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 220b, compare “Phlb.” 67a.}}
**B.II** **remove, depart from,** ἐκ τῆς χώρης, ἐξ Αἰγύπτου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61, 2.139, +others; μαντικῶν μυχῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 180; γῆς ἀπαλλάσσεσθαι πόδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 729; δόξης, δέους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42; ἀ. παρά τινος 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.78}}; **depart, go away,** ἐς τὴν ἑωυτοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82, +others; ἐπὶ τῆς ἑωυτοῦ [prev. author] 9.11, compare 5.64; πρὸς χώραν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 938a: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93, +others, Aen. Tact. 10.19, 15.9.}}
**B.II.2** ἀπαλλάσσεσθαι τοῦ βίου **depart from** life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 102, “Hipp.” 356; βίου ἀπαλλαγὴν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 496e}}; frequently without τοῦ βίου, **depart, die,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 1000, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81c, etc.}}
**B.II.3** ἀ. λέχους **to be divorced,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 592; ἀ. γυναῖκά τε ἀπ᾽ ἀνδρὸς καὶ τὸν ἄνδρα ἀπὸ γυναικός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 868d.}}
**B.II.4** ἀ. τῶν διδασκάλων **leave** school, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 514c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.24.}}
**B.II.5** ἀ. ἐκ παίδων **become a man,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.40.}}
**B.II.6** **to be removed from, free from** the imputation of, ἀπηλλαγμένος εὐηθίης **many removes from** folly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60; ξυμφορῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122; αἰσχύνης [prev. author] 3.63: with}}_infinitive_, κρῖναι ἱκανῶς οὐκ ἀπήλλακτο **was** not **far from** judging adequately, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.138.}}
**B.II.6.b** πολλὸν ἀπηλλαγμένος τινός far **inferior to** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.144.}}
**B.II.7** **depart from, leave off from,** τῶν μακρῶν λόγων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1335; σκωμμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 316; ἀ. λημμάτων}}**give up the pursuit of..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.33}}; οὐκ ἀπήλλακται γραφικῆς **is** not **averse from..,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 35.}}
**B.II.7.b** _absolutely_, **have done, cease,** of things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 422; ὅταν ἡ μέθη ἀπαλλαγῇ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1202a3.}}
**B.II.7.c** **throw up one's case, give up a prosecution,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.151,198.}}
**B.II.7.d** with _participle_, εἰπὼν ἀπαλλάγηθι speak and **be done withit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 491c, compare “Tht.” 183c; ταῦτα μαντευσάμενος ἀπαλλάττομαι [prev. author] “Ap.” 39d; ἀπαλλάχθητι πυρώσας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 600}}: also in _participle_, with a Verb, οὐκοῦν ἀπαλλαχθεὶς ἄπε; **make haste** and begone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 244.}}
**B.II.8** **to depart from enmity,** i.e. **to be reconciled, settle a dispute,** πρὸς ἀλλήλους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 915c: _absolutely_, [prev. work] 768c.}}
**B.II.9** **recover from an ailment,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.14.}}

526:
** ἀπαλλοτρι-όω**,
_perfect_ ἀπηλλοτρίωκα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.194}}:—**estrange, alienate,** ἀφ᾽ ὑμῶν τὸν ἐπὶ Θρᾴκης τόπον [same place]; λόγον -οῦντα κακίας{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Aristo Stoic. 1.80; τινά τινος 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.1.1}}:—_passive_, **to be alienated,** τινός **from** one, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.79.6, compare 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Topicorum libros octo commentaria - pars” 389.12}}; πρός τινα towards one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 7.13, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.48; ἀπηλλοτριωμένην πρὸς φυτείαν ἀμπέλου χώραν}}**ill-suited for..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.73.}}
**2** of property, **alienate,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1361a22, “IPE” 12.32 B 68 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.1157v iii3 (3rd c.AD).}}
**3** of things, **separate, distinguish,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 58 (_passive_).}}
**3.b** **alter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 65d (_passive_).}}
**3.c** **remove,** in Surgery, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 46.26.13 (_passive_)}}:—_active_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.789.}}

527:
** ἁπᾰλός**, ή, όν,
**soft to the touch, tender**: in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly of the human body, ἁπαλὴν ὑπὸ δειρήν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.371; παρειάων ἁπαλάων 18.123; ἁπαλοῖο δι᾽ αὐχένος ἦλθεν ἀκωκή 17.49; ἁ. πόδες 19.92}}; ἁ. τέ σφ᾽ ἦτορ ἀπηύρα, i. e. the life of **young** animals,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.115; ἵεσαν αὐδὴν ἐξ ἁπαλῶν στομάτων 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 279; δέρα 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.16}}; of persons, **delicate,** παῖδες{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.5; εὐμορφοτέρα.. τᾶς ἀπάλας Γυρίννως 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 76}}; of flowers, ἄνθρυσκα {{Refs|Ead. “Supp.” 25.13}}; rare in Trag., and only in Lyric poetry, παρειά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 70; βρέφος ἁ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1285; βλέφαρον τέγγουσ᾽ ἁ. “El.” 1339; more frequently in Comedy texts, σισύμβριον 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 239; κρέα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1063; δάκτυλοι 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 48; θερμολουσίαις ἁπαλοί “Comica Adespota” 56}}; so in Prose, ἁ. ψυχή{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245a}}; of **raw** fruit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.18}}; of **tender** meat, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.5.2}}; **soft-boiled,** of eggs, [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|{5th c.AD}: Caelius Aurelianus Medicus “Anthologia Graeca” 2.18}}]; of a **gentle** fire, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 79.8, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.25.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **soft, gentle,** ἁπαλὸν γελάσαι laugh **gently,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.465}}; ἁ. δίαιτα **soft,** **delicate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239c; τῶ αὐτῷ.. χρησώμεθα τεκμηρίῳ περὶ Ἔρωτα ὅτι ἁπαλός [prev. author] “Smp.” 195e (also in _superlative_)}}; ἁ. εἴσπλους λιμένος, opposed to τραχύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 357.}}_adverb_ ἁπαλῶς, ὀπτᾶν to roast **moderately,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Sotades Lyricus Comedy texts 1.16}}: _comparative_ ἁπαλωτέρως, ἅπτεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 37.}}
**II.2** in bad sense, **soft, weak,** ὡς ἁ. καὶ λευκὸς [οἶνος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 183; λευκός, ἐξυρημένος, γυναικόφωνος, ἁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 192}}. [ᾰπᾰλο; for καλάμῳ.. ὑπᾱπάλῳ, in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.4, is corrupt.]}}

528:
** ἀπαντάω**,
_imperfect_ ἀπήντων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.127}}, _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers.singular_ ἀπάντη{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 9.7}}: _future_ ἀπαντήσω{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1432b35, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.26.5, etc.}} but better **-ήσομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.77, 7.2 and 80, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.3, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.32, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἀπήντησα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1392, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.20}}: _perfect_ ἀπήντηκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 420, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.15}}:—the _middle_, used in _active_ sense by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 1.21.1 (_imperfect_), +others, is censured by 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Legal.” 25}}; so also _perfect_ ἀπήντημαι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.37.6, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.88, etc.}}
**I** mostly of persons, **move from** a place **to meet** a person, and generally, **meet, encounter,** τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.9, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 772, etc.; ἐξ ἐναντίας ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 893e}}; ἀ. ταῖς ὁμοίαις φύσεσι **encounter, fall in with** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.20: _absolutely_, ὁ ἀεὶ ἀπαντῶν}}**any one that meets you, any chance person,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 563c; οἱ ἀπαντῶντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.45, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 78, compare 87.}}
**I.b** frequently with a _preposition_, ἀ. τινὶ εἰς τόπον **come** or **go** to a place **to meet** him, **meet** him at a place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.75; ἐς τωὐτό 6.84; ἐπὶ Τριποδίσκον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.70; τὸν μὲν ἐς τὰς Σίφας ἀ., τὸν δ᾽ ἐπὶ τὸ Δήλιον [prev. work] 89}}: without _dative_ _person_, **present oneself** at a place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.1; εἰς Κύζικον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.3.13; ἀ. ἐνθάδε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 13; δεῦρο πάλιν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 210d, etc.}}
**I.c** with _dative_ loci, **light upon, come to,** τόπῳ{{LXX|LXX.Gen.28.11.}}
**I.2** frequently in hostile sense, **meet** in battle, ἀ. δορί (_dative_ _person_ being omitted) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1392; ὅπλοις “HF” 542; τοῖς βαρβάροις Μαραθῶνάδε 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.107; ἀ. Ἀθηναίοις ἐς Τάραντα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34, compare 2.20, 3.95; ἀ. πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.86,90}}; generally, **resist, oppose** in any way, νομοθέτῃ ἀ. λέγων..{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 684d}}; διὰ λόγων νουθετικῶν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|probably [prev. work] 740e; ἀ. τραχέως πρὸς τὰς τῶν πλησιαζόντων ὀργάς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.31; ἀ. τοῖς εἰρημένοις}}**rejoin, reply,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 11.30; τοῖς θορύβοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rh.Al.” [same place]; πρὸς ἕκαστον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.24.}}
**I.2.b** _absolutely_, **present oneself** in arms, **attend the muster,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 782.}}
**I.2.c** **face, meet,** αἰκίαις καὶ θανάτοις{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Hecataeus Abderita Historicus cited in 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 1.22.}}
**I.3** frequently as a law-term, **meet in open court,** τῷ καλεσαμένῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 937a, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.3, etc.: without _dative_ _person_, ἀ. πρὸς τὴν δίκην}} **present oneself** at the trial, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936e}}; πρὸς ἣν [δίκην] οὐκ ἀπήντα **did** not **appear to defend** his cause, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.90}}; ἀ. πρὸς τὸν διαιτητήν, etc., **come before** him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 40.11, etc.; εἰς ἡμέραν τὴν συγκειμένην ἀ. εἰς τὸ Ἡφαιστεῖον 33.18; ἐπὶ τὰ ἱερά 42.7; ἐπὶ τὴν δίαιταν Test. cited in [same work] 21.93}}; ἀ. ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀλλοτρίοις ἀγῶσι **to be present at** other people's suits, **meddle with** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.205}}: _absolutely_, **appear in court,** {{Refs|40.16, etc.}}
**I.4** ἀ. εἰς.. **enter into** a thing, **attempt** it, εἰς τὸν ἀγῶνα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 830a}}; ἀ. εἰς τὴν τίμησιν **come to** the question of rating, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.198; ἀ. εἰς τὰς χρείας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1158a8; ἀ. πρὸς τὰς μαθήσεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 144b}}; πρὸς τὴν ἐρώτησιν, τὸ πρόβλημα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1036a14, “Ph.” 213b3}}; ἀ. πρὸς τὴν τροφήν **go to seek** it, {{Refs|[prev. author]}}**de.An.**{{Refs|421b12; ἀ. ἐπί..}}**have recourse to..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.151, 24.193, etc.; ἐπὶ ταύτας τὰς οἰκίας ἀ. οἱ τραγῳδοποιοί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1454a12.}}
**II** of things, **come upon** one, **meet** or **happen to** one, ἀ. δάκρυά μοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 940, compare 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus [same place]; τοῖς πρὸς ὑμᾶς ζῶσι τοσαύτην κωφότητα.. παρ᾽ ὑμῶν ἀπαντᾶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.226}}; ἐπὶ τῶ κεφαλαίῳ τῶν πραγμάτων ἀ. [ἡ ῥᾳθυμία] 'comes home to roost',{{Refs 5th c.BC+|10.7; ἀ. αὐτῷ κραυγὴ παρὰ τῶν δικαστῶν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.163; μή τίς σοι ἐναντίος λόγος ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101a, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.33, etc.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **happen, occur, turn out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 420, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 358e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1302a6, “Top.” 160a23, +others; τούτων ἀπαντώντων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.142}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.7.4, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.9.}}

529:
** ἀπάντ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
= {ἀπάντημα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 828, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.10U., 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.26.8, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.59}}; εἰς -σίν τινι ἐξελθεῖν{{LXX+NT|LXX.Judg.11.31, NT.Matt.25.6}}; **escort,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.43.3, etc.}}
**II** **meeting** in **argument, reply,** πρός τι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 176a23, “Metaphorical” 1009a20, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 19, 28}}; ἀ. ποιεῖσθαι to **reply,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.63.7}}; προσφιλὴς κατ᾽ ἀ. in **conversation,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 10.5.6, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.13J., [prev. author] “Herc.” 1457.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.803f(_plural_).}}
**III** **steadfastness in face of opposition,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 5.}}

530:
** ἅπαξ** [ᾰπ],
_adverb_ **once, once only, once for all,** first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, ὅτε τ᾽ ἄλλοι ἅ. θνῄσκουσ᾽ ἄνθρωποι 12.22}}; ἅ... ἀπὸ θυμὸν ὀλέσσαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 350; ἀπαλλάχθηθ᾽ ἅ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 600}}; οὐχ ἅ. μόνον more than **once,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 211; ἅ... κοὐχὶ δίς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1208; πολλάκις καὶ οὐχὶ ἅ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46; πολλάκις τε κοὐχ ἅ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1275; μὴ ἅ. ἀλλὰ πολλάκις 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711a; μὴ δίς, ἀλλ᾽ ἅ. μόνον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1299a10}}; of the self-creation of Νοῦς, τὴν ποίησιν αὑτοῦ.. ἅ. εἶναι{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.8.21}}; ἅ. ἔτι **yet** this **once,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1322}}; τὸ ἅ. τοῦτο **at** this **moment,** {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.17.7}}; ἅ. δυοῖν ποδοῖν, i.e. **two** square feet {{Refs|(1 x 2), opposed to δυοῖν δίς (2 x 2)}}**four,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 82c.}}
**2** with _genitive_, ἅ. τοῦ ἐνιαυτοῦ, ἔτεος ἑκάστου ἅ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.59, 4.105; also ἐν τῷ ἐνιαυτῷ ἅ. [prev. author] 2.132.}}
**3** **once on a time, formerly,** ἅ. καὶ ἅ.{{LXX|LXX.Judg.20.30.}}
**II** without any notion of number, after conditional and temporal Particles, if **once,** when **once,** εἴπερ ἐσπείσω γ᾽ ἅ. if **once** you have made a treaty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 307. compare 923; ἢν ἅ. ἁλῷ [prev. author] “V.” 898, compare “Av.” 342; ἂν ἅ. τις ἀποθάνῃ 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 8; ἐπειδήπερ γ᾽ ἅ. ἐμοὶ σεαυτὸν παραδέδωκας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1129; ἐπεὶ ἅ. ἐταράχθησαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44; ὡς ἅ. ἤρξατο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.58; ἐπεὶ ἅ. αὐτοῖς φίλος ἐγένετο [prev. author] “An.” 1.9.10, compare 3.2.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.242; ὡς ἅ. ἐγκλήματα ἐταράχθη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.151}}: so with _participle_, ἐπὶ γᾶν ἅ. πεσὸν... αἷμα{{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1019(Lyric poetry); ἅ. θανόντος οὔτις ἔστ᾽ ἀνάστασις [prev. author] “Eu.” 648; ἅ. ἐλθόντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 165e, cf. NT.Heb.6.4, etc. (ἁ-}} = **sṃ** (compare εἷς); -παξ akin to πήγνυμι.)

531:
** ἀπαρά-βᾰτος**, ον,
**unalterable,** εἱρμὸς αἰτιῶν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.266; ἐπιπλοκή, of causation, Chrysipp.[prev. work]293; τάξις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.410f; ἡ τῆς κινήσεως ἰδέα 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 1.15}}; **infallible,** προρρήσεις{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 28.135, compare 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 4.14}}; also of persons, {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).215.}}_adverb_ -τως Chrysippsipp.{{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.279.}}
**2** **inviolable,** κύρια καὶ ἀ.{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PRyl.” 65.18 (1st c.BC),compare “PGrenf.” 1.60.7 (6th c.AD).}}
**3** **permanent, perpetual,** ἱερωσύνη{{NT|NT.Heb.7.24.}}
**3** _active_, **not transgressing,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.8.2; ἀ. τῶν καθηκόντων 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 10p.435M.}} _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.15.1.}}

533:
** ἀπαρν-έομαι**,
_future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 461c}}: _aorist_ ἀπηρνησάμην{{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 75, 107, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1133 (variant), NT.Matt.16.24, +others}}, but in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, **-ηρνήθην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 480, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1266, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.56, etc.}}:—**deny utterly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.69; κλέψαντες ἀπαρνεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.4; μή.. ἀπαρνηθεὶς γένῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217c}}; ἀπαρνηθῆναί τι **to refuse, reject** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus}} [same place], etc.; ἀ. μή with _infinitive_, τὸν.. ἀπαρνηθέντα μὴ χρᾶναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same place]}}; οὐκ ἀπαρνοῦμαι τὸ μή (i.e. δρᾶσαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 443, “Aj.” 96; τίνα οἴει ἀπαρνήσεσθαι μὴ οὐ..; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 461c; οἷός ἐστι μὴ ἂν ἀ. χαρίσασθαι [prev. author] “Phdr.” 256a.}}
**2** in Logic, opposed to κατηγορεῖν, **deny,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 41a9:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 63b37.}}
**3** ἀ. ἑαυτόν **deny** oneself, {{NT|NT.Matt.16.24, +others}}
**II** _passive_, _future_ ἀπαρνηθήσεται it **shall be denied** or **refused,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 527, cf. NT.Luke.12.9, uncertain in LXX.Isa.31.7}}: _aorist_ ὥνθρωπος ἓν μὲν οἶδεν, ἓν δ᾽ ἀπηρνήθη **was refused,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.74.}}

534:
** ἀπαρτί** [ῐ],
_adverb_ **completely,** and, of numbers, **exactly, just,** ἡμέραι ἀ. ἐνενήκοντα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.53, compare 2.158; φρόνιμος ὢν ἀ. ταύτης τῆς τέχνης 5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 37; ἀ. ἐναρμόζειν πρός τι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 73: of Time, ἀ. ἐν καιροῖσι.. [prev. author] “Acut.” 41.}}
**II** **just the contrary,** τί.. ἀποτίνειν τῷδ᾽ ἀξιοῖ;—ἀ. δή που προσλαβεῖν παρὰ τοῦδ᾽ ἐγὼ μᾶλλον **on the contrary,** I expect to receive.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 93, compare 71, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 388.}}
**III** ἀπάρτι, properly ἀπ᾽ ἄρτι, of Time, **from now, henceforth,** {{NT|NT.Matt.23.39, etc.}}
**III.2** **just now, even now,** {{NT|NT.John.13.19, etc.}} (This is not an _Attic dialect_ use, hence {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 143}} must be incorrectly interpretation by {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 79.)}}

535:
** ἀπαρτ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**completion,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.14.28; ἔργων “PGiss.” 67.9 (2nd c.AD)}}; λογοθεσίας Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 88i see 25 (2nd c.AD)}}; κατ᾽ ἀπαρτισμόν **precisely,**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.164}}; οὐ κατ᾽ ἀπαρτισμὸν ἀλλ᾽ ἐν πλάτει **not narrowly but broadly,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 24.}}
**2** **rounding off,** βαλάνου{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 50.3.1.}}

536:
** ἀπαρχή**, ἡ,
mostly in _plural_ ἀπαρχαί (compare ἄπαργμα):
**1** **beginning of a sacrifice, primal offering** (of hairs cut from the forehead), ἀπαρχαὶ κόμης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 96, compare “Ph.” 1525 (Lyric poetry)}}; later, a **banquet** held on this occasion, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.40b.}}
**2** **firstlings** for sacrifice or offering, **first-fruits,** ἁπάντων ἀπαρχαί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.71; ἀπαρχὰς ἄγειν θεοῖσι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 183; ἀπαρχὰς θύειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 516; ἀ. σκυλευμάτων “Ph.” 857; ἐπιφέρειν ἀ. τῶν ὡραίων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58; τῶν ὄντων 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.42, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 130, etc.}}:—so also in _singular_, λείας ἀ.{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 761; ἀ. τῶν πατρων χρημάτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.92, etc.; ἀνθρώπων ἀ. εἰς Δελφοὺς ἀποστέλλειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 485; ἀ. ἀπό τινος ἀνατιθέναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.88; inscribed on votive offerings, [ἀνέθηκεν].. τόδ᾽ α. “IG” 1.382, etc.; frequently in LXX, as LXX.Exo.25.2, +others, cf. NT.Rom.11.16, and _metaphorically_, ἀ. τῶν κεκοιμημένων NT.1Cor.15.20; τῶν κτισμάτων NT.Jam.1.18.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, ἀπαρχαὶ τῶν ἐμῶν προσφθεγμάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 402; ἀπαρχὴν τῆς σοφίας ἀνέθεσαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 343b, etc.; ἀ. ἀπὸ φιλοσοφίας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.172c.}}
**4** **tax on inheritances,** {{Refs|PTaur. 1.7.10}}; **tax** paid by Jews, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Stud.Pal.” 4.72 (1st c.AD).}}
**5** **entrance fee,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PTeb. 316.10 (1st c.AD), +others}}
**6** **board of officials** (compare {ἀπάρχης}), {{Refs|“IG” 12(8).273 (from Thasos).}}
**7** **birth-certificate** of a free person, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PTeb.” 316.10 (1st c.AD), “PGnom.” 131 (2nd c.AD)}}: perhaps _metaphorically_ in {{NT|NT.Rom.8.23.}}

537:
** ἅπᾱς**, ἅπᾱσα
(also ἅπανσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 56.25 (Argos, 5th c.BC))}}, ἅπαν (ἅ- = **sṃ-,** compare εἷς), strengthened for πᾶς, **quite all, the whole,** and in _plural_ **all together,** frequently from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards; ἅπασι}}**in all things,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1; τοῖσι ἅπασι 91; ἐν ἅπασι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 156; ἐφ᾽ ἅπασι 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.365.}}
**2** with _adjective_, ἀργύρεος δὲ ἔστιν ἅ. **all** silver, i.e. of **massive** silver, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.616, 15.116; ἅ. δὲ τραχὺς ὅστις ἂν νέον κρατῇ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 35; μικκός γα μᾶκος.. ἀλλ᾽ ἅπαν κακόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 909, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.19, 148}}; ἡ ἐναντία ἅπασα ὁδός the **exactly** contrary way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317b.}}
**3** with abstract _substantive_, **all possible, absolute,** ἅπασ᾽ ἀνάγκη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 171; σπουδή 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.23; ἀτοπία 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 39.1.7; εἰς ἅπαν ἀφικέσθαι ἀνοίας 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 7.15.8.}}
**4** sometimes with _Article_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 483, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in _singular_, **every one,** _neuter_ **everything,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 108b}}; οὐ πρὸς [τοῦ] ἅπαντος ἀνδρός not in the power of **every** man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.153; οὐκ ἐξ ἅπαντος δεῖ τὸ κερδαίνειν φιλεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 312}}; ἐξἅπαντος εὖ λέγει **in any cause whatever,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 807; σῖγα νῦν ἅπας ἔχε σίγαν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 144}}; ἅπαν γένοιτ᾽ ἂν ἤδη **nihil non..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 528}}: with _substantive_, ἅπαντι λόγῳ in **every** matter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 231}}; τὸ ἅπαν, as _adverb_, **altogether,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241b}}; καθ᾽ ἅπαν as **a whole,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96d; ἐς ἅπαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.103; εἰς ἅπαν}}**at all,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 18.266; πρὸς ἅπαν 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.493; ἐξ ἅπαντος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 41. [ᾰπᾰν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.185, etc., 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.49}}; but ᾰπᾱν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 129, 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus 57, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.56, and _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to Hdn.Gr. 2.12}}; ᾰπᾰν in _anapaest meter_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 493: the use of ἅπας for πᾶς is chiefly for the sake of euphony after consonants.]}}

539:
** ἀπᾰτ-η**, ἡ,
**trick, fraud, deceit,** νῦν δὲ κακὴν ἀπάτην βουλεύσατο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.114, compare 4.168}}: in _plural_, **wiles,** οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἔμελλες.. λήξειν ἀπατάων, says Athena to Ulysses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.294, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.31; σκολιαὶ ἀπάται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 213.}}
**2** **guile, treachery,** ἄταν ἀπάτᾳ μεταγνούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 111, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 230; ἀπάτης δικαίας οὐκ ἀποστατεῖ θεός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 301, compare “Pers.” 93; ἀ. ἐρώτων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 617; διαβολὴ καὶ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.7, etc.}}; ἀ. εὐπρεπής, opposed to βία ἐμφανής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86; ἢ βίᾳ ἢ ἀπάτῃ 2.39}}; ἀ. λεχέων **a being cheated out of** the marriage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 630}}; ἄνευ δόλου καὶ ἀπάτης 'without fraud or **covin',** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.69; μετὰ σκότους καὶ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 864c.}}
**3** Ἀπάτη, personified, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 224, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 42.}}
**II** **beguiling** of time, **pastime** (not _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 65), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.20.5; ψυχῆς 4th c.BC: Dicaearchus Geographus 1.1; ψυχαγωγίαι καὶ ἀπάται τῆς πόλεως 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 32.5.}}
**III** as name of a plant, _falsa lectio_ for{ἀπάπη} (which see).

540:
** ἀπάτωρ** [πᾰ], ορος, ὁ, ἡ,
(πατήρ) **without father,** of deities, αὐτοπάτωρ, ἀ.{{NT+4th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 10.10; ἀ... ἀμήτωρ 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 41.53, cf. NT.Heb.7.3}}; **fatherless, orphan,** ἀοίκους ἀπάτοράς τε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 300; ἀμήτωρ ἀ. τε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 109 (Lyric poetry); ἀ. πότμος [prev. author] “IT” 864 (Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 103.35: _neuter_ _plural_, ἀπάτορα τέκεα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 114 (Lyric poetry)}}; **disowned by the father,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 929a: also with _genitive_, ἀ. ἐμοῦ}} **not having** me **for a father,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1383.}}
**2** **of unknown father,** like{σκότιος}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.288e, “PGrenf.” 2.56.3 (2nd c.AD).}}

542:
** ἀπεῖδον**,
_infinitive_ ἀπῐδεῖν, _aorist 2_ with no _present_ in use, ἀφοράω being used instead:— **look away from other things at,** and so simply, **look at,** πρός or ἔς τι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 9.2, +others; πόρρωθεν ἀπιδεῖν 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 21.}}
**II** **look away from,** and so, **despise,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1070f (uncertain reading). (In later Greek ἀφ-, ἀφίδω NT.Phil.2.23.)}}

544:
** ἀπειθ-έω**,
_Attic dialect_ form of ἀπῐθέω (though even Trag. preferred ἀπιστέω, which see 11), **to be disobedient, refuse compliance,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1049}}; opposed to πείθομαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 271b}}: frequently with _dative_, **disobey,** οὐκ ἀπειθήσας θεῷ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 31; ἀ. ἅμα νόμῳ καὶ τῷ θεῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 741d, etc.}}; τὰ μεγάλα ἀ. τινί **in** great matters, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 538b}}; ἀ. ταῖς ἐνεχυρασίαις **not to abide by** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 949d: later with _genitive_, ψαφίσματος “GDI” 3705.111 (from Cos)}}; ἐντολῶν{{LXX|LXX.Josh.5.6.}}
**2** of animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.62}}; of ships, τοῖς οἴαξιν ἀ.{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.46.}}
**3** of a woman, **refuse compliance,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.20.}}

545:
** ἀπειθής**, ές,
**disobedient,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 45; ἀ. τοῖς νόμοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936b}}; of ships, τοῖς κυβερνήταις ἀπειθεστέρας τὰς ναῦς παρεῖχον **less obedient to** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 50.29 (_comparative_), 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 247; στράτευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 3.6}}; of horses, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 1.3}}; ἀπειθέα τεύχειν work **disobedience,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 66}}. _adverb_ ἀπειθῶς, ἔχειν πρός τινα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 391b.}}
**b** **unbelieving,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 8.306.}}
**2** of things, **inflexible, rigid,** κέντρον{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 1.55; σιδήρου καὶ ἀδάμαντος ἀπειθέστεροι 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.87; ὀδόντες ἀ.}}**unyielding,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 2.511}}; χῶρος ἀ. **impracticable,** of Hades, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.3; δίκη ἀ. Νεμέσεως “IG” 4.444.}}
**II** _active_, **not persuasive, incredible,** μῦθος{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1235}}; **uninviting,** πρὸς τὴν γεῦσιν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Hicesius Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.87b, c; τὴν γεῦσιν [prev. author] 323a}}; of places, **difficult of access,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 120.}}

546:
** ἀπειλέω**,
**B**_3rd.pers.dual_ _imperfect_ _active_ ἀπειλήτην, _Epic dialect_ for ἠπειλείτην, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.313}}: later _Epic dialect_ _present_ ἀπειλείω {{Refs 4th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Musaeus Epicus 122, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 20.204}}:— **hold out** either in the way of **promise** or **threat,** and therefore:
**B.I** sometimes in good sense, **promise,** οὐδ᾽ ἠπείλησεν ἄνακτι.. ῥέξειν κλειτὴν ἑκατόμβην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.863, compare 872}}; also, **boast** or **brag,** ὥς ποτ᾽ ἀπειλήσει.1; ἦ μὲν ἀπείλησας βητάρμονας εἶναι ἀρίστους{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.383, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.57a.}}
**B.II** commonly in bad sense, **threaten,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus either _absolutely_, as 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.665, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.368: or (more frequently) with _dative_ _person_, [prev. work] 20.272, etc.: c._accusative_ _cognate_, αἶψα δ᾽ ἀναστὰς ἠπείλησεν μῦθον}} **spake a threatening** speech, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.388}}; ἀπειλὰς ἀ., see at {ἀπειλ; δείν᾽ ἀπειλήσων ἔπη} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 542}}: frequently with _neuter_ _pronoun_ or _adjective_, ἀ. τόγε θυμῷ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.212}}; ταῦτα, πολλὰ ἀ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.18, 1.111, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.33, etc.; πύργοις ἀ. δεινά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 426; τοῦτ᾽ ἀπειλήσας ἔχεις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 817.}}
**B.II.2** with _accusative_ of the thing threatened, θάνατον ἀ. ὃς ἂν..{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.81; ξίφος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 47; ζημίας ἀ. κατά τινος [prev. author] “Cam.” 39}}; ἠπείλησαν τοὺς ἄρχοντας **threatened** **them with** the prefects, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 47.7.}}
**B.II.3** dependent clauses were added in _future_ _infinitive_, γέρας.. ἀφαιρήσεσθαι ἀπειλεῖς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.161, compare 15.179, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.313; σφέας.. ἀπείλεε ἐκτρίψειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37; ἀ. δράσειν τι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 287; ἀ. ἀποκτενεῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.28}}: rarely in _present_ _infinitive_, ἠπ... ἑλκέμεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.682: after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in _aorist_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.4, “HG” 5.4.7, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.16.}}
**B.II.4** ἀ. ὅτι.., ὡς.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 88, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.22, etc.}}; ἀ. τινί, εἰ μή.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.5.12.}}
**B.III** _passive_, ἀπειλοῦμαι, of persons, **to be terrified by threats,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 4.31.}}
**B.III.2** of things, τὰ ἀπειληθέντα, ={ἀπειλαί}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 823c.}}
**B.IV** later in _middle_, with _aorist 1_ **-ησάμην**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.29, 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 7.35.2}}: with _infinitive_, **forbid with threats,** ἀπειλησώμεθα αὐτοῖς μηκέτι λαλεῖν{{NT|NT.Act.4.17.}}

547:
** ἀπειλ-ή**, ἡ,
mostly in _plural_, **boastful promises, boasts,** ποῦ τοι ἀπειλαὶ ἃς.. ὑπίσχεο οἰνοποτάζων;{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.83; μέχρι τῶν ἀπειλῶν γενναῖος 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 59.118, compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 704.28.}}
**II** commonly in _plural_, **threats,** ποῦ τοι ἀπειλαὶ οἴχονται τὰς Τρωσὶν ἀπείλεον υἷες Ἀχαιῶ;{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.219; οὐδὲ.. λήθετ᾽ ἀπειλάων τὰς ἀντιθέῳ Ὀδυσῆι πρῶτον ἐπηπείλησεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.126, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.200, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.32; εὐθύνειν ἀπειλαῖς καὶ πληγαῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325d, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 175 (Lyric poetry): in _sing._, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 753, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126; ἀπειλῆς ἕνεκα τοῖς ἐν τῷ Ταρτάρῳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 94b33.}}
**II.2** of threatening conditions, ἀ. πνιγμοῦ{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 2.60}}; τὰ ἐν ἀπειλῇ ἀποστήματα Heras cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.815}}; of storms, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.21.5, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.7.}}

548:
** ἄπειμι**,
(εἰμί
**A** **sum**), _imperfect_ ἀπῆν (later ἀπήμην{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1204.23 (3rd c.AD))}}, _2nd pers.singular_ ἀπῆσθα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 379}}; _Epic dialect_ ἀπέην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.7, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἄπεσαν [prev. work] 10.357}}: _future_ ἀπέσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 887}}; _Epic dialect_ ἀπέσσομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.150, _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἀπεσσεῖται [prev. work] 19.302}}:—**to be away** or **far from,** with _genitive_, ὁππότε πάτρης ἧς ἀπέῃσιν ἀνήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 169, compare 20.155, +others; ἐὰν δ᾽ ἀπῇ τούτων τὸ χαίρειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1169; ἀ. ἀπὸ τῶν ἰδίων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141: with _dative_, φίλοισιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 179 (Lyric poetry), compare “Tr.” 393, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.1, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61, etc.: but mostly}}
**A.2** _absolutely_, **to be away** or **absent,** and of things, **to be wanting,** οἵ τ᾽ ὄντες οἵ τ᾽ ἀπόντες, i.e. all that are, every one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1109; τὰς οὔσας τέ μου καὶ τὰς ἀπούσας ἐλπίδας [prev. author] “El.” 306}}; of the dead, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 312}}; τοῦ θεοῦ ἀπεόντος the god **not being counted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.53:—in _3rd.pers._ _plural_}}_imperfect_, ἀπῆσαν and ἀπῇσαν are frequently confused in codices, as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.42.}}

549:
** ἄπειμι**,
(εἶμι
**B** **ibo**), serving as _future_ of ἀπέρχομαι: _infinitive_ ἀπιέναι, in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.404 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus) ἀπίναι}}:—**go away, depart,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.593, +others}}; οὐκ ἄπε; ={ἄπιθι}, **begone,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 431; ἀπιὼν οἴχεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.65, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.43; οἱ πρέσβεις περὶ τῶν σπονδῶν ἀπῇσαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.39}}; of soldiers, ἀπίασι οὐδενὶ κόσμῳ **will retreat,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.60}}.; ἀ. πρὸς βασιλέα **desert** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.29}}; ἀ. πάλιν **return,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.4.7, compare 15}}; ἄπιτε ἐπὶ τὰ ὑμέτερα αὐτῶν **return** to your homes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.97; ἄπιμεν οἴκαδε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 255; ἀπῇσαν ἐπ᾽ οἴκου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.36; εἰς τὴν πατρίδα 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.23.36}}; of the Nile, **recede.** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.108}}; ἀ. ἐπί τι **go** in quest of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.80}}; μηνὸς ἀπιόντος, for the common φθίνοντος, Canon Laws texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.37, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3658 (from Cyzicus)}}; **die,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 15, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.9.}}
**B.2** with _accusative_ _cognate_, πολλὴν καὶ τραχεῖαν ἀπιέναι (i.e. ὁδόν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 272c.}}
**B.3** **to be discharged,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 8, +others}}

550:
** ἀπεῖπον**,
_infinitive_ ἀπειπεῖν, _Epic dialect_ ἀπο?~Xειπεῖν, ἀπο?~Xειπέμεν, and _participle_ ἀποειπών, i.e. ἀποϝειπών, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.35, etc.}}: less frequently _aorist_ I ἀπεῖπα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 405:}}_future_ in use is ἀπερῶ, _perfect_ ἀπείρηκα, mostly in _signification_ {{Refs|IV. 3}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ I ἀπειπάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, 5.56, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1163b19}}, but never in correct _Attic dialect_:—_passive_, _future_ ἀπορρηθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.14}}: _aorist_ ἀπερρήθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 929a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.21:}} —_present_ and _imperfect_ are supplied by ἀπαυδάω, ἀπόφημι, and in _Attic dialect_ Prose by ἀπαγορεύω:—**speak out, tell out, declare,** μῦθον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.309, compare 431; ἀγγελίην 7.416; ἀληθείην 23.361; ἐφημοσύνην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.340}}; μνηστήρεσσιν ἀπειπέμεν (probably μνηστῆρσ᾽ ἀποειπέμεν) **to give** them **full notice,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.91; ἀπηλεγέως ἀποειπεῖν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.309, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.373}}; ῥῆσιν ἀπερέοντα **to deliver** a **verbal** message, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.152}}: in _aorist_ _middle_, ἀπείπασθαι θανάτῳ ζημιοῦν τοὺς πλευσομένους{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 837a2.}}
**II** **deny, refuse,** ὑπόσχεο καὶ κατάνευσον, ἢ ἀπόειπ᾽{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.515, compare 9.510, 675}}; σύμφαθι ἢ ἄπειπε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 523a: with _accusative_, βοήθειαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 2.}}
**III** **forbid** {{Refs|compare (ἀπαγορεύω 1), frequently in Prose, ἀ. τινι μὴ ποιεῖν}} **forbid** one **to** do, **tell** him **not to** do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1760, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 556}}; also without μή, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.52.8}}: with _infinitive_ omitted, ἀπειπὼν εἴργει μελάθρων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1333, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 405}}; ἀ. τινί τι **forbid** him **the use of** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1264a21}}:—_passive_, ἀπειρῆσθαι γάρ οἱ.. μηδενὶ ἐπιδεικνύναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.61}}; τὸ ἀπειρημένον a **forbidden** thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.52, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.7; ἀπείρηται δὲ τοῦτο τῷ νόμῳ 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 7.7}}; ἀπειρημένον, _absolutely_, **contrary to orders,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1373b10.}}
**IV** **renounce, disown, give up,** with _accusative_ _of things_, ἀ. μῆνιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.35}}; and not seldom in Prose, as εἴτε.. ἀπερέουσι.. τὴν συμμαχίην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.205}}; ἀπειπεῖν.. κηρύκων ὕπο.. πατρῴαν ἑστίαν **renounce** it by public proclamation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 737; τὸν υἱὸν ὑπὸ κήρυκος ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 928d; πόνους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1354; προξενίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.43, 6.89; ὁμιλίαν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.6; ταῦτα μὲν οὖν ἀπείποι τις ἄν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.113}}; ἀ. τὴν στρατηγίαν **to resign** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.41; τὴν ἀρχήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1272b5; ἐλπίδας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.9.6}}; ἀ. γυναῖκα **divorce** her, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 38:}} —in _passive_, αἱ σπονδαὶ οὐκ ἀπείρηντο **had** not **been renounced,** remained in force, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.48; τὰς σπονδὰς μέλλειν ἀπορρηθήσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.14}}:—in _middle_, ἀπείπασθαι παῖδα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59; συμμαχίην 4.120, 125; φιλίαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 33.10.5; ἀ. ὄψιν}}**averruncare,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.56}}; ἀ. υἱόν, πατέρα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1163b19}}; ἀποϝειπάθθω (Cretan dialect for ἀπειπάσθω) **let him renounce** the inheritance, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 11.11}}; ἀ. γνώμας **withdraw, retract** them, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 8.}}
**IV.2** **refuse,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 4.30.}}
**IV.3** _intransitive_, **fail, tire, sink from exhaustion,** ἐπεὶ δ᾽ ἀπεῖπε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 789, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 306, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228b, etc.; ἀπειρηκὸς σῶμα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.93; γῆ ἀπειρηκυῖα 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.20.3}}; οὐ γάρ που ἀπεροῦμέν γέ πω **shall** not **give in** yet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 200d; ἕως ἂν ἀπείπωσιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.25, compare 27; οἱ διὰ τὸν χρόνον ἀπειρηκότες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1329a33}}; οὐδ᾽ ἀπεῖπεν.. φάτις **was** not **unfulfilled,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 840.}}
**IV.3.b** with _dative_ _person_, **fail** or **be wanting to** one, οὐκ ἀ. φίλοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 459.}}
**IV.3.c** with _dative_ _of things_, **fail** or **fall short in** a thing, ἀπειρηκότων δὲ χρήμασι **now that they are bankrupt,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.8; ἀ. σώμασι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.92, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 40}}; but,
**IV.3.d** ἀ. κακοῖς, ἄλγει, **give way to, sink under** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 91, “Hec.” 942; ἀ. ὑπὸ πλήθους κακῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.15.}}
**IV.3.e** ἀ. πρὸς τὸν φόνον **to be tired** of butchery, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 18; ἀ. ἐν τοῖς δράμασι 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 191, 14.}}
**IV.3.f** with _present_ _participle_, ἀ. ταλαιπωρούμεναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 778}}; φέροντες ἀπεροῦσιν they **will be tired of** paying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.121}}; ἀ. λέγων **give over** speaking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 769e}}; ἀπειρήκη τὰ ὄντα σκοπῶν I **had failed to..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 99d, etc.}}

551:
** ἀπείραστος**, ον,
**incapable of being tempted,** κακῶν{{NT|NT.Jam.1.13.}}
**II** **without experience,** τῶν ἀβουλήτων{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.37.}}
**III** **not experienced,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.459}}; **untried,** τέχνη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.45 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}

552:
** ἄπειρος**, ον,
(πεῖραρ, πέρας)
**B** **boundless, infinite,** σκότος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 130.8; τὸν ὑψοῦ τόνδ᾽ ἄ. αἰθέρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 941; ἤπειρον εἰς ἄ. [prev. work] 998}}; of number, **countless,** πλῆθος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.204; ἀριθμὸς ἄ. πλήθει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 144a; ἄ. τὸ πλῆθος [prev. author] “R.” 525a, +others; εἰς ἄ. τὴν ἀδικίαν αὐξάνειν [prev. author] “Lg.” 910b; χρόνος ἄ. “OGI” 383.113 (1st c.BC)}}: _comparative_ **-ότερος**{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 50, 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteorologicorum librum primum commentarium - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 17.15}}; τὸ ἄ. **the Infinite,** as a first principle, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 203a3, etc.}}; especially in the system of Anaximander, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.1, etc.}}; but τὰ ἄπειρα **individuals,** opposed to τὰ εἴδη, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 109b14, compare “Metaphorical” 999a27, +others}}; ἄπειρος, opposed to πεπερασμένος, {{Refs|“Ph.” 202b31}}; εἰς ἄ. ἰέναι, προϊέναι, ἥκειν, etc., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“APo.” 81b33, “Ph.” 209a25, “EN” 1113a2, etc.; [γῆ] ἐπ᾽ ἄπειρον ἐρριζωμένη 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.1.20}}; also, **indefinite,** ὕλη{{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.86.}}
**B.2** in Trag., frequently of garments, etc., **in which one is entangled past escape,** i.e. **without outlet,** ἀμφίβληστρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1382; χιτών 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 526; ὕφασμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 25.}}
**B.3** **endless,** i.e. **circular,** ἀ. δακτύλιος a simple hoop-ring, ={ἄλιθος} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.179), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 207a2; compare ἀπείρων (B) 1.3.}}_adverb_ -ρως, θρυφθῆναι **into an infinite number of** fragments, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 899b16.}}

553:
** ἀπεκ-δέχομαι**,
**expect anxiously, await eagerly,** σωτῆρα{{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Phil.3.20; θάνατον 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.7; τὸ μέλλον 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.35, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 2.73.}}
**II** **misunderstand, misinterpret,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.6.11, +others}}
**II.b** **understand** a word **from** the context, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 226.20.}}

554:
** ἀπεκ-δύομαι**,
_future_ -δύσομαι [ῡ]: _aorist 1_ -εδῡσάμην:— **strip off oneself**: _metaphorically_, **put off,** τὸν παλαιὸν ἄνθρωπον{{NT|NT.Col.3.9.}}
**II** **strip off for oneself, despoil,** τινά {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.15.}}

556:
** ἀπελαύνω**,
also ἀπέλα as _imperative_ from _present_ ἀπελάω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.32}}; _Doric dialect_ _imperfect_ ἀπήλαον _vulgar form_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1001 (but probably ἀπήλααν}} ={ἀπήλασαν}, should be read): _future_ **-ελάσω**{{LXX|LXX.Eze.34.12}}; _Attic dialect_ -ελῶ (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.102)}}: _perfect_ **-ελήλακα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.10:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ -ηλάθην [ᾰ]: _perfect_ _participle_ ἀπεληλαμένος{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 4 “Prooem.”}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ **-ηλασάμην**{{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.303 (Antip. Sid.)}}:—**drive away, expel from** a place, τινὰ δόμων, πόλεως, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 553, etc.; ἀπὸ τόπου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.16}}; ἀ. τινά **drive away, banish** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 93, 1356, etc.}}; **expel** (from a society), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.32}}; **exclude, keep at a distance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 58}}; **remove,** φόβον τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.10}}; **exclude from** a thing, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 3.2.31:}} —_middle_, ἀ. τί τινος **ward off, avert from** him, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” [prev. cited]}}
**2** ἀ. στρατιήν **lead away** an army, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.92: frequently _absolutely_ like{ἀπάγω}}}], **march, depart,** ἐς τὰς Σάρδις{{Refs|[prev. author] 1.77, compare 5.25, etc.; πυρώσας τὰς Ἀθήνας ἀπελᾷς [prev. author] 8.102}}; also (i.e. ἵππον) **ride away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 9.7, etc.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be driven away,** ἐνθεῦτεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.94; ἐντεῦθεν εἰς ἄλλον τόπον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.3; γῆς ἐμῆς πρός τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 599}}; **to be excluded from** a thing, ἁπάσης [τῆς στρατιῆς] **from** the command, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.161, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.15; τῆς πολιτείας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.5; τῶν ἀρχῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 564d}}; ἀ. τῆς φροντίδος **to be far** **from,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.205}}; ἐς πατέρ᾽ ἀπηλάθην τύχης **was barred from** [good] fortune on my father's side, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 63; ἀ. φιλίας 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 7.90c.}}

560:
** ἀπελπ-ίζω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ **-ιῶ**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.50:}}_perfect_ -ήλπικα:—**despair of,** τῆς πόλεως τὴν σωτηρίαν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 35; τὸ μέλλον 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.62U.; πράξεις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.19.12, etc.}}:—_passive_, **to be given up in despair,** τὰ πράγματα{{Refs|[prev. author] 10.6.10}}; of persons, **to be despaired of,** οἱ ἀπηλπισμένοι{{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Isa.29.19, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.5.2; ὑπὸ τῶν ἰατρῶν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.25, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.69, compare “IG” 14.966}} (ἀφηλπ-).
**2** ἀ. τινός **despair of,**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.55.2, +others}} οὐκ ἀ. τινός **to be confident of,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.365; περὶ τῆς νίκης 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.25.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **hope** that a thing will **not** happen, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.59.}}
**II** causal, **drive to despair,** τινά{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.114 (1st c.AD: Nicarchus Epigrammaticus).}}
**III** **hope to receive back,** μηδὲν (variant{μηδένα}) ἀπελπίζοντες{{NT|NT.Luke.6.35 (uncertain).}}

561:
** ἀπέναντι**,
_adverb_, (ἔναντι) **opposite,** with _genitive_, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.86.3, LXX.Gen.21.16, “PGrenf.” 1.21.14 (2nd c.BC), “IG” 2.489b17, NT.Matt.27.61}}; **before, in the presence of,** ἀ. ὑμῶν{{NT|NT.Act.3.16}}; **against,** with _genitive_, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.37.4, NT.Act.17.7.}}
**2** _absolutely_, εἰς τὸν ἀ. βουνόν{{Refs|“Inscription Prien.” 37.168.}}
**3** ={κατὰ ἀνατολὰς νότου}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

562:
** ἀπέραντος**, ον,
(περαίνω) **boundless, infinite,** of space, πεδίον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.38 (who also has ἀπείραντος ἀλκά “P.” 9.35); πόντου κλῇδ᾽ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 213 (Lyric poetry); τὸν ἀέρα τόνδ᾽ ὄντ᾽ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 393; ὁδός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147c}}; of time, **endless,** τὸ χρῆμα τῶν νυκτῶν ὅσον· ἀπέραντον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 3; χρόνος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302a}}; of number, **countless, infinite,** ἀ. ἀριθμὸς ἀνθρώπων{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Criti.” 119a; ἀ. κακά [prev. author] “R.” 591d, +others; λῦπαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 7; πένθη “Fab.” 17}}; **unlimited,** τιμωρίαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.39}}; generally, of events, business, etc., ἀπέραντον ἦν there was **no end to it,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.36; μακρὸν καὶ ἀ. φαίνεται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101a26}}; ἀπέραντα περαίνειν represent as concluded **what is not concluded,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 9}} (with allusion to _signification_ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|111); μηδὲν ἀβασάνιστον μηδ᾽ ἀ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.75.3.}}_adverb_, τὸ ἀπεράντως διεστηκός of **unlimited** dimensions, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 204b21, “Metaphorical” 1066b33.}}
**II** **allowing no escape, whence none can pass,** Τάρταρος, δίκτυον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 153, 1087 (both Lyric poetry).}}
**III** in Logic, **inconclusive,** λόγος{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.97 W., compare “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.77.}}
**IV** **incomplete, imperfect,** of persons, ἀτελὴς καὶ ἀ.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.12.}}

564:
** ἀπερί-τμητος**, ον,
**uncircumcised,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Gen.17.14, +others, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.1.2: _metaphorically_, καρδία LXX.Eze.44.7,cf. NT.Act.7.51, +others}}
**II** **not clipped** or **circumscribed,** φύσις{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.495c.}}

565:
** ἀπέρχομαι**,
_future_ -ελεύσομαι (_Attic dialect_ _future_ ἄπειμι): _perfect_ -ελήλυθα: _aorist_ -ῆλθον:— **go away, depart from,** with _genitive_, πάτρης{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.766; οἴκου 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.136, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1165, etc.; λόγου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 546; ἀ. ἀπὸ τοῦ βουλευτηρίου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92; ἐκ τῆς χώρας [prev. author] 1.89, etc.: _metaphorically_, ἀ. ἐκ δακρύων}} **cease from** tears. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 205.}}
**2** with εἰς, implying **departure from** one place **and arrival at** another, ἀ. ἐς τὰς Σάρδις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22; ἀ. εἰς Θουρίους οἰκήσοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.12; παρά τινα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 11}}; ἀ. ἐπ᾽ οἴκου **depart** homewards, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.92; ἀθῷος οἴκαδε 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 40}}; ἀπῆλθεν ὅθεν.. **went back to** the place whence he came, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 481.3: _metaphorically_, ἀ. εἰς τὴν ἀρχαίαν φύσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193c.}}
**3** _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199, etc.}}; ταχεῖ᾽ ἀπέρχεται (i.e. ἡ νόσος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 808}}; κᾷτ᾽ ὀφλὼν ἀ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 689}}; ἄπελθε τουτονὶ λαβών take him and **be off!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Av.” 948; ἀπελθόντος ἐνιαυτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954d; νυκτὸς -ομένης 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 315.}}
**4** with _participle_, ἀ. νικῶν **come off** conqueror, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.2 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 7,etc.}}
**5** **spread abroad,** ἀπῆλθεν ἡ ἀκοὴ αὐτοῦ εἰς Συρίαν{{NT|NT.Matt.4.24.}}
**II** **depart from life,** ἀ. κάτω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 379, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 818(Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 3.6, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.335, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.513, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.7.15; εἰς τοὺς θεούς “PPetr.” 2p.45 (3rd c.BC).}}

566:
Included with: ** ἀπέχω**,
_future_ ἀφέξω, and {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.572) ἀποσχήσω:}}_aorist_ ἀπέσχον:— **keep off or away from,** αἴ κεν τυδέος υἱὸν ἀπόσχη Ἰλίου ἱρῆς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.96, 277; ἑκὰς νήσων ἀπέχων εὐεργέα νῆα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.33}}; κληῖδες ἀπ᾽ ὤμων αὐχέν᾽ ἔχουσιν the collar-bone **parts** the neck **from** the shoulders, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.324}}; Εὐβοίης ἀπέχειν.. αἶγας Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.20, compare 22; ἄπεχε τῆς βοὸς τὸν ταῦρον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1125 (Lyric poetry), compare “Pr.” 687 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** with _dative_ _person_, τοι.. χεῖρας ἀφέξω{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.263.}}
**3** with a _preposition_, ἀ. φρένα περισσῶν παρὰ φωτῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 427 (Lyric poetry); ῥῖνα ἀπὸ κάκκης 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 162.}}
**4** c._accusative_ only, **keep off** or **away,** σκοτεινὸν ἀ. ψόγον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Pi.N. 7.61; ἀ. φάσγανον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1519.}}
**5** οὐδὲν ἀπέχει with _infinitive_, nothing **hinders, debars** one **from** doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Cra.407b, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.433a.}}
**II** _middle_, κακῶν ἄπο χεῖρας ἔχεσθαι **hold one's** hands **off or away from..,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.316; κυάμων ἄπο χεῖρας ἔχεσθε 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 141; ἀθανάτων ἀ. χεῖρας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 350 (Lyric poetry), compare “Supp.” 756, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 213d, 214d:}} —but mostly,
**II.2** ἀπέχεσθαί τινος **hold oneself off** a thing, **abstain** or **desist from** it, πολέμου{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.35, +others; βοῶν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.321}}; οὐδὲ.. σευ ἀφέξομαι will not **keep my hands off** thee,{{Refs|[prev. work] 19.489; Δεκελέης}}**abstain from ravaging** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 0.73, compare 1.66, 4.118, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20, etc.}}; **keep away from,** πόλεως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.10}}: in _perfect_ _passive_, μηδὲ τῶν μικρῶν ἀπεσχημένον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.47; ἀγορᾶς ἀπεσχ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278a25.}}
**II.3** with _infinitive_, ἀπέχεσθαι μὴ στρατεῦσαι **abstain from** marching, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.25; λαμβάνειν ἀπέσχετο 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 94.3; ἀπέχεσθαι τοῦ ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.3; οὐκ ἀ. τὸ μὴ οὐ ποιεῖν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.32, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 354b}}: also with _participle_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.43d.}}
**II.4** _absolutely_, **refrain oneself,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.61.}}
**III** _intransitive_ in _active_, **to be away** or **far from,** with _genitive_ loci, τῶν Ἐπιπολῶν ἓξ ἢ ἑπτὰ σταδίους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.97}}; ἀ.ἀπὸ Βαβυλῶνος, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179,compare 3.26, +others; ἀπὸ θαλάττης.. δώδεκα δδὸν ἡμερῶν ἀ. 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 11.3; ἀ. παμπόλλων ἡμερῶν ὁδόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.3; τὸ μέσον ἴσον τῶν ἐσχάτων ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 145b; πλεῖστον ἀ. κατὰ τόπον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 363a31; a). τὴν ἡμίσειαν διάμετρον [prev. author] Cael. 293b30, etc.}}
**III.b** **project, extend,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 781a11; τὰἀπέχοντα}}**prominent parts, PA** {{Refs|655a32.}}
**III.c** ἀποσχὼν τεσσαράκοντα μάλιστα σταδίους μὴ φθάσαι ἐλθών**failing** to arrinein time by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.3.}}
**III.2** of actions, **to be far from,** ἀπεῖχον τῆς ἐξευρέσιος οὐδὲν ἔλασσον **were** just **as farfrom** the discovery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.67; τοσοῦτον ἀπέχω τοῦ ποιεῖν τι ὥστε.. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.70; τοσούτῳ πλέον ἡμῶν ἀπέχεις τοῦ πιστὰ λέγειν ὅσον.. [prev. work] 11.32}}; ἀπέχει τοῦ μὴ μετ᾽ ὀργῆς [πράττειν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.41}}; πλεῖστον ἀ. τινός **to be as far** as possible **from** doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.62}}; but τοσοῦτ᾽ ἀπέχει τῶν χορηγῶν so far **is it from the thoughts of..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.59.}}
**III.3** generally, **to be far remoued from,** πολιτείας, μοναρχίας, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1289b2, 1293a17; τοῦ μέσου [prev. author] “EN” 1109a10.}}
**III.4** **differ from,** οὐδέν τι ἀπεῖχε γαμετῆς γυναικός{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.16.4.}}
**III.5** διαφύσιες.. ᾗσιν οὐδὲν ἀπέχει ἀγγεῖα εἶναι nothing **is wanting,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 10.}}
**IV** **have** or **receive in full,** τὴν ἀπόκρισιν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.50}}; τὸ χρέος ἀ. **receive** payment **in full,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 55; χάριτας [prev. work] 51}}; ἀπέχω in receipts, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 612.2 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; ἀ. τὸν μισθόν{{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 22, NT.Matt.6.2, +others; καρπὸν ἀ. τῶν πονηθέντων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 17; ἀ. τὸ μέτεριον [prev. author] 2.124e.}}
**IV.2** _impersonal_, ἀπέχει **it sufficeth, it is enough,** {{NT|NT.Mark.14.41, compare “Anacreontea” 15.33.}}

