569:
** ἀπιστ-έω**,
_future_ _passive_ ἀπιστηθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 32.10, but ἀπιστήσομαι in _passive_ sense, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450c}}:—**to be** ἄπιστος, and so:
**I** **disbelieve, distrust,** ἐγὼ τὸ μὲν οὔ ποτ᾽ ἀπίστεον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.339; τύχην ἀ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1130; πάντα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 775, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.28 (assuming variant), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 5.6, 8.7:}} —_passive_, τὴν γνῶσιν τοῦ οἰκείου ἀπιστεῖσθαι **was distrusted,** i.e. no one could be sure of knowing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44; ἀ. ἐν μαρτυρίαις 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.7; ἐπειδὰν γνῶσιν ἀπιστούμενοι, οὐ φιλοῦσι τοὺς ἀπιστοῦντας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.17, compare “Hier.” 4.1; ὑπὸ τῆς πατρίδος [prev. author] “Smp.” 4.29, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.49:}} —but mostly,
**I.2** with _dative_ _person_, κρατέουσιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 5.1, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.83, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319b, etc.: so with _dative_ _of things_, τῷ χρησμῷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.158; πῶς ἀπιστήσω λόγοι; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1350, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.86; ἀ.τῇ ἐξ αὑτῶν ξυνέσει [prev. author] 3.37; τοῖς ἰάμασιν “IG” 4.951.24 (Epid.); ἡμῖν αὐτοῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1112b10}}; ἀ.τινί τι **disbelieve** one in a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.122; περί τινος [prev. author] 4.96}}; οἷς ἠπίστησαν ἔχειν.. whom they **suspected of** having, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.18.8.}}
**I.3** c._infinitive_, οὐδέν σ᾽ ἀπιστῶ καὶ δὶς οἰμῶξαι I nothing **doubt** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 940}}; ἀ. μὴ γενέσθαι τι **to doubt** that it could be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10; ἀπιστοῦντες αὐτὸν μὴ ἥξειν [prev. author] 2.101, compare 4.40, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 301c, “R.” 555a; also ἀ. μὴ οὐκ ἐπιστήμη ᾖ ἡ ἀρετή [prev. author] “Men.” 89d; ἀ. πῶς.. “Phd.” 73b; ἀ. εἰ.. “APl.” 4.52 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus), 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.555; ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89d; ὡς “R.” 450c:}} —_passive_, τὸ ἐπιτήδευμα ἀπιστεῖται μὴ δυνατὸν εἶναι it **is not believed** to be possible, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 839c, compare “Chrm.” 168e.}}
**I.4** _absolutely_, **to be incredulous,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.94, cf. NT.Mark.16.16, etc.; νᾶφε καὶ μέμνασ᾽ ἀπιστεῖν 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 250; ἐπὶ τοῖς λεγομένοις 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.92.}}
**II** ={ἀπειθέω}, **disobey,** τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108, frequently in Trag.and 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 640, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 381 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 1183, 1224, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29c, +others}}: _absolutely_, **to be disobedient,** τοῖς ἀπιστοῦσιν τάδε **in** these things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 219, compare 656}}; ἢν δ᾽ ἀπιστῶσι but if they **refuse to comply,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 389, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 941c.}}
**II.2** **to be faithless,** εἰ ἡμεῖς ἀπιστοῦμεν, ἐκεῖνος πιστὸς μένει {{NT|NT.2Tim.2.13.}}
**II.3** νεκρὸς τὸν θάνατον ἀπιστούμενος **belying** death, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Callimachus” 55.}}
**III** τὸ σῶμ᾽ οὐκ ἀπιστήσω χθονί, i.e. {{Refs|1}}**will** not **hesitate to commit** it.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 1024; τούτῳ ἠπίστησεν ἀποθανοῦσαν ἑαυτὴν ἐπιτρέψαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.21.}}

570:
** ἀπιστία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ίη, ἡ, **unbelief, distrust,** πίστεις.. δμῶς καὶ ἀπιστίαι ὤλεσαν ἄνδρας beliefs and **disbeliefs,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 372; πίστει χρήματ᾽ ὄλεσσα, ἀπιστίῃ δ᾽ ἐσάωσα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 831 [ῑ]; τῶν θείων τὰ πολλὰ ἀπιστίῃ διαφυγγάνει μὴ γινώσκεσθαι 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 86, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 493c; τοῖσι παρεοῦσι ἀ. πολλὴ ὑπεκέχυτο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.66, compare 2.152; ὑπὸ ἀπιστίης [prev. author] 3.153, +others}}; ὑπὸ ἀ. μὴ γενέσθαι τι from **disbelief** that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.68}}; ἀ. πρὸς ἑαυτόν **lack of** self-**confidence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66; ἀπιστίᾳ λόγους ἐνδέχεσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1606; πέφευγε τοὔπος ἐξ ἀπιστίας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 268}}; ἀπιστίαν ἔχειν περί τινος to be **in doubt,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107b; σώφρων ἀ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1617}}; πρὸς -ίαν τοῦ κατηγόρου to **discredit** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1398a10; ἡ ἀ. ἡ πρὸς ἀλλήλους [prev. author] “Pol.” 1297a4; ἀ. ἡ καθ᾽ αὑτοῦ 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 38.2; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 258c.}}
**2** of things, τὰ εἰρημένα ἐς ἀ. πολλὴν ἀπῖκται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193}}; πολλὰς ἀπιστίας ἔχει it admits of many **doubts,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450c; ὁ λόγος εἰς ἀ. καταπίπτει [prev. author] “Phd.” 88d; καταβαλεῖν τινὰ εἰς ἀ. [prev. work]c; ἀ. παρέχειν [prev. work] 86e (interpolated)}}; ἀτοπία καὶ ἀπιστία **incredibility,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.48; ταῦτ᾽ ἀπιστίαν ἔχει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.44.}}
**II** **want of faith, faithlessness,** θνήσκει δὲ πίστις βλαστάνει δ᾽ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 611}}; **treachery,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.21; βλέπειν ἀπιστίαν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 309.}}

571:
** ἄπιστος**, ον,
**I** _passive_, **not to be trusted,** and so:
**I.1** of persons and their acts, **not trusty, faithless,** ὑπερφίαλοι καὶ ἄ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.106; θεοῖσίν τ᾽ ἐχθρὲ καὶ ἀνθρώποισιν ἄπιστε 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 601; ἄ. ὡς γυναικεῖον γένος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1298; ἄ. ληίστορες “Sammelb.” 4309.14 (3rd c.BC)}}; δολοπλοκίαι{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 226; ἄ. ποιεῖν τινά}}**mistrusted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22, compare 9.98; τὰ ἑαυτοῦ πιστὰ ἀ. ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.7; ἄπιστος ἑταιρείας λιμήν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 683}}; θράσει ἀ. ἐπαιρόμενος by **untrustworthy, groundless** confidence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120}}; **shifty, unreliable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 775d.}}
**I.2** of reports and the like, **incredible,** uncertain in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.5, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.31, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80; τέρας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 832; ἄ. καὶ πέρα κλύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 418; ἄ. ἐνόμιζον εἰ.. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.556}}; τὸ ἐλπίδων ἄ. **undreamed of** even in hope, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 868}}: _comparative_ -ότερος, λόγος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.59}}: _superlative_, πίστις ἀπιστοτάτη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.67, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 314b.}}
**II** _active_, **mistrustful, incredulous, suspicious,** θυμὸς δέ τοι αἰὲν ἄ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.150; ὦτα.. ἀπιστότερα ὀφθαλμῶν}}**less credulous,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8}}; ἄ. πρὸς Φίλιππον **distrustful** towards him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.27}}; ἄ. εἶ.. σαυτῷ you do **not believe** what you say yourself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26e; ἤθη ἄ. [prev. author] “Lg.” 705a}}; τὸ ἄ., ={ἀπιστία}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66; δούλοις πῶς οὐκ ἄπιστο; 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Pal.” 11.}}
**II.b** in **NT, unbelieving,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.6.6, +others}}
**II.2** **disobedient, disloyal,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 627: with _genitive_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 876}}; ἔχειν ἄπιστον.. ἀναρχίαν πόλει, i.e. ἀναρχίαν ἔχειν ἀπειθοῦσαν τῇ πόλει,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1035, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1476.}}
**III** _adverb_ ἀπίστως:
**III.1** _passive_, **beyond belief,** ἀ. ἐπὶ τὸ μυθῶδες ἐκνενικηκότα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.21}}; οὐκ ἀ. not **incredibly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1438a22, 1438b2.}}
**III.2** _active_, **distrustfully, suspiciously,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83; ἀ. τινὰ διαθεῖναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.22.}}
**III.2.b** **treacherously,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.516.}}

572:
** ἁπλότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**singleness,** τῆς φωνῆς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 801a19.}}
**II** **simplicity,** πόλεως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.18; κατὰ τὴν μουσικήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 404e; τῆς τροφῆς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.17: of literarystyle, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 9.14: _plural_, ἁπλότητες λόγων [prev. passage]}}
**II.2** of persons, **simplicity, frankness, sincerity,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.3, LXX.Wis.1.1, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.78.8, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.66, etc.; ἡ εἰς τὸν Χριστὸν ἁ. NT.2Cor.11.3.}}
**II.3** **open-heartedness**: hence, **liberality,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 8.2, 9.11, compare “IG” 14.1517.}}

573:
Included with: ** ἁπλόος**, η, ον,
_contraction_ ἁπλοῦς, ῆ, οῦν, opposed to διπλόος **twofold,** and so,
**I** **single,** ἁπλῆ γὰρ οἶμος εἰς Ἁιδου φέρει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 239, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.4 (_comparative_)}}; ἁπλῷ τείχει περιτειχίζειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.18; δὶς τόσ᾽ ἐξ ἁπλῶν κακά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 277; ὅπως ἂν ἡ χάρις ἐξ ἁπλῆς διπλῆ φανῇ [prev. author] “Tr.” 619; ἁπλᾶς δὲ λύπας ἐξὸν οὐκ οἴσω διπλᾶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 688.}}
**I.b** ἁπλαῖ (i.e. κρηπῖδες), αἱ, **single-soled shoes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 24, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.34.}}
**II** **simple, plain, straightforward,** κελεύθοις ἁπλόαις ζωᾶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.36; ἁ. ὁ μῦθος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 554; ἁ. λόγῳ [prev. author] “Pr.” 610, +others}}; ὡς ἁ. λόγῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 46, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1151}}; ἁ. λόγος the matter is **simple,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 979}}; ἁ. διήγησις **simple** narrative (without dialogue), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 392d}}; οὐκ ἐς ἁπλοῦν φέρει leads to no **simple issue,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 519; ἁπλᾶ γε καὶ σαφῆ λέγω μαθεῖν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 240.7; οὐδὲν ἔχω ἁπλούστερον λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.32}}; of **single-membered** periods, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 17, etc.}}; of habits, ἁπλούστατος βίος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.10.5; νόμοι λίαν ἁ. καὶ βαρβαρικοί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1268b39; ἁπλοῦν ἦν.. ἀποθανεῖν}}**a plain course,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 14.}}
**II.b** of persons, or their words, thoughts, and acts, **simple, open, frank,** ἁπλᾶ γάρ ἐστι τῆς ἀληθείας ἔπη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 176; ἁ. καὶ γενναῖος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361b, etc.; ἁ. τρόποι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 927}}; opposed to δόλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1158; πρὸς τοὺς φίλους ὡς ἁπλούστατον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.16.}}
**II.c** **simple-minded,** ὁ κριτὴς ὑπόκειται εἶναι ἁ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1357a12, compare “HA” 608b4 (_comparative_), “Rh.” 1367a37}}; in bad sense, **simple, silly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.46; λίαν γὰρ ἁπλοῦν τὸ νομίζειν.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 339b34.}}
**III** **simple,** opposed to **compound** or **mixed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 547e, etc.}}; opposed to μεμιγμένος, κεκραμένος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 989b17, “Sens.” 447a18; ἁ. χρώματα [prev. author] “Col.” 791a1}}; ἁ. ὀνόματα, opposed to διπλᾶ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1457a31}}; also of nouns, **without the article,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 98.17, +others}}; of the **positive** adjective, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.412e, etc.}}
**III.b** ἁ. βιβλία rolls **containing a single author,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 58.}}
**III.c** of precious metals, **unalloyed, pure**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“SIG” 901.9 (Delph., 4th c.AD), “PCair.” 67041.}}
**III.d** ἁ. ἐπίδεσμος, a kind of **bandage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 7, etc.}}
**III.2** **absolute, sheer,** ἀκρασία{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1149a2; συμφορά 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.8, etc.}}
**III.3** **simple, unqualified** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ἁπλῶς 11.3), οὐ πάνυ μοι δοκεῖ.. οὕτως ἁπλοῦν εἶναι ὥστε.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 331b, compare “Smp.” 206a, “Tht.” 188d, +others}}
**III.4** **general,** opposed to ἀκριβής, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1025b7 (_comparative_), compare 1030a16.}}
**IV** _adverb_ ἁπλῶς, see entry _vocative_
**V** _comparative_ and _superlative_ ἁπλούστερος, ἁπλούστατος, see above; irregular _superlative_ ἁπλότατος{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.185 (1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus)}}. (Cf. δι-πλόο; ἁ- = **sṃ;** -πλόος perhaps identical with πλοῦς 'voyage', cf. Serb. **jedan put** '(**one journey,** hence) **once';** transition from 'once' to 'simple' as in Lett. **vienkars?~Xs** 'simple' (cf. Lithuanian _vienkart_ 'once').)

574:
** ἁπλ-ῶς**,
_adverb_ of ἁπλοῦς, **singly, in one way,** μένειν ἁ. ἐν τῇ αὑτοῦ μορφῇ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 381c, etc.}}; ἁ. λέγεσθαι **in one sense,** opposed to πολλαχῶς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 158b10}}; ἁ. λεγόμενα, opposed to συμπλεκόμενα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1014a19, compare “Ph.” 195b15}}; opposed to κατ᾽ ἀλλήλων λέγεσθαι, **without distinction of subject and predicate, Metaphorical**{{Refs|1041b1}}; ἐσθλοὶ μὲν γὰρ ἁ. παντοδαπῶς δὲ κακοί Poët. cited in {{Refs|“EN” 1106b35, etc.}}
**II** **simply, plainly,** ἀλλ᾽ ἁ. φράσον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 464; ἁ. τι φράζουσ᾽ [prev. author] “Ch.” 121; ἁ. εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.154; λαλεῖν 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.23.}}
**II.b** **openly, frankly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.52, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.37}}; **in good faith,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.308, etc.: in bad sense, ἁ. ἔχειν}} to be **a simpleton,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.16.}}
**II.c** **in its natural state, uncooked,** of food, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.192b.}}
**II.2** **simply, absolutely,** ἁ. ἀδύνατον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45}}; τῶν νεῶν κατέδυ οὐδεμία ἁ. no ship was **absolutely** sunk (though some were disabled), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.34; ἁ. οὐδὲ ἕν.. συνίημι 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 123}}; ὅσ᾽ ἐστὶν ἀγαθὰ.. ἁ. **simply** all the good things there are, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 873; ἔδωκ᾽ ἐμαυτὸν ὑμῖν ἁ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.179}}; ἁ. **absolutely,** opposed to κατά τι (**relatively**), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 115b12}}; opposed to πρός τι, {{Refs|“APr.” 41a5}}; opposed to πρὸς ἡμᾶς, {{Refs|“APo.” 72a3}}; opposed to τινί, {{Refs|“Top.” 116a21}}; ἁ. βαρύ, κοῦφον, μαλακόν, etc., {{Refs|“Cael.” 311a17, 27, “Mete.” 386b32, +others}}; τὸ ἁ. καλόν, τὸ ἁ. ἀγαθόν, etc., {{Refs|“EN” 1136b22, 1134b4, +others}}; opposed to ὁτιοῦν (**in some particular**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Pol.” 1301a29; strengthened ἁ. οὕτως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 468c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.99}}; τὴν ἁ. δίκην **absolute, strict** justice, opposed to τοὐπιεικές and χάρις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 770; ἡ τελεία καὶ ἁ. κακία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1138a33}}; τὸ ἁ. the **absolute,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 5}}: _comparative_ ἁπλούστερον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.2}} **-τερως** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.4}}: _superlative_ ἁπλούστατα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 921b.}}
**II.3** **in a word,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 851, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.33, “Mem.” 1.3.2, etc.}}
**II.4** **generally,** opposed to σαφέστερον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1341b39, +others}}; ὡς ἁ. εἰπεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1285a31, “EN” 1115a8, +others; ἁ. δηλῶσαι “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 11.4; τὸν ἀκριβῶς ἐπιστάμενον λέγειν ἁ. οὐκ ἂν δυνάμενον εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.11, compare 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 100, 243}}: in bad sense, **loosely, superficially,** λίαν ἁ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987a21, “GA” 756b17, +others}}; οὐχ ἁ. φέρειν not **lightly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 899.}}
**II.5** **foolishly,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.72b.}}

575:
** ἀπό**,
_Aeolic dialect_, Thess., {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 5th c.AD: Cypria ἀπύ 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 44, compare 78, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 33, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.16, “IG” 12(2).6.45 (Mytil.), ἀπυδόμεναι [prev. work]9(2).594 (from Larissa), 5(2).6 (from Tegea)}}, etc.:—_preposition_ usually with Gen. but see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}} (Cf. Sanskrit
**A** **ápa,** Latin _ab,_ Umbr. **ap-ehtre** 'ab extra', Gothic _af,_ O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **af, cef, of,** etc.) Orig. sense, **from.** [ᾰπο?~X: where ἀπο ¯ is found in _Epic dialect_ before see or liquids (as ἀπὸ ἕθεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.62, ἀπὸ νευρῆς 11.664, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 409) ἀπαί}} was sometimes written in later texts,{{Refs|compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 625.11:}} — ᾱ for the sake of meter in _Epic dialect_ compounds, such as ἀπονέεσθαι.]
**A.I** OF PLACE, the earliest, and in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus the prevailing sense:}}
**A.I.1** of Motion, **from, away from,** ἐσσεύοντο νεῶν ἄπο καὶ κλισιάων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.208}}; pleonastic, ἀ. Τροίηθεν{{LXX+8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 24.492; ἀπ᾽ οὐρανόθεν 8.365 (later with _adverbs_, ἀπὸ ἔμπροσθεν LXX.Eccl.1.10, etc.); strengthened, ἐκτὸς ἀ. κλισιης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.151}}; also ἀπ᾽ αἰῶνος νέος ὤλεο, implying **departure from** life, {{Refs|[prev. work] 24.725}}; opposed to ἐξ, of relatively superficial motion, λαμβάνομεν οὔτε ἐκ τῆς γῆς οὐδέν, οὔτ᾽ ἀπὸ τῶν οἰκιῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.2}}; similarly of the cause or ground, ἐξ ὧν προηγώνισθε καὶ ἀφ᾽ ὧν εἰκάζω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126}}:— frequently of warriors fighting **from** chariots, etc., οἱ μὲν ἀφ᾽ ἵππων, οἱ δ᾽ ἀ. νηῶν.. μάχοντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.386; ἀφ᾽ ἵππων μάρνασθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.49; so ἡ μάχη ἦν ἀφ᾽ ἵππων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79}}; λαμπὰς ἔσται ἀφ᾽ ἵππων **on** horse**back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328a; ἀφ᾽ ἵππου θηρεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.7; ἀ. νεῶν πεζομαχεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.62; ἐν ταῖς ναυσὶν αἰρόμενος τοὺς ἱστοὺς ἀ. τούτων ἐσκοπεῖτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.29}}; ὀμμάτων ἄπο.. κατέσταζον γένυν, of tears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 240: joined with ἐκ, ἐκ Κορίνθου ἀ. τοῦ στρατοπέδου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142a.}}
**A.I.2** of Position, **away from, far from,** μένων ἀ. ἧς ἀλόχοιο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.292 (compare ἀπ᾽ ἀνδρὸς εἶναι}} to live **apart from** a man or husband, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 4); κεκρυμμένος ἀπ᾽ ἄλλων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.110; μοῦνος ἀπ᾽ ἄλλων “hymnus ad Mercurium” 193}}; ἀπ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν, ἀπ᾽ οὔατος, **far from** sight or hearing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.53, 18.272, compare 22.454; ἀ. θαλάσσης ᾠκίσθησαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.7, compare 46; αὐλίζεσθαι ἀ. τῶν ὅπλων [prev. author] 6.64; ἀπ᾽ οἴκου εἶναι [prev. author] 1.99}}; σπεύδειν ἀ. ῥυτῆρος **far from,** i.e. **without using** the rein, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 900; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently strengthened, τῆλε ἀ..., νόσφιν ἀ..., 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.880, 5.322}}; in measurement of distances, ὅσον ιέ στάδια ἀ. Φυλῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.4, etc.}}; but later the numeral follows ἀ., πηγὰς ἔχων ἀ. μ σταδίων τῆς θαλάσσης{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.56; ἀ. σταδίων κ τῆς πόλεως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 4}}; κατεστρατοπέδευσεν ἀ. ν σταδίων fifty stades **away,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Oth.” 11, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 17.17.}}
**A.I.3** of the mind, ἀ. θυμοῦ **away from,** i. e. **alien from,** my heart, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.562; ἀ. δόξης 10.324; οὐ.. ἀ. σκοποῦ οὐδ᾽ ἀ. δόξης 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.344; ἀ. τοῦ ἀνθρωπείου τρόπου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76}}; οὐδὲν ἀ. τρόπου not **without** reason, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 470b}}; οὐκ ἀ. σκοποῦ, καιροῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 179c, 187e; οὐκ ἀ. γνώμης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 389; οὐκ ἀ. τοῦ πράγματος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.6; μάλα πολλὸν ἀπ᾽ ἐλπίδος ἔπλετο 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.863.}}
**A.I.4** in pregnant sense, with Verbs of rest, previous motion being implied (compare ἐκ), ἀνὰ δ᾽ ἐβόασεν.. ἀ. πέτρας σταθείς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 523}}; ἀ.τῆς ἐμῆς κεφαλῆς τὴν [ἐκείνου] κεφαλὴν ἀναδήσω, i. e. taking the chaplet **off** my head, and placing it on his, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 212e: with Verbs of hanging, where ἐκ}} is more common, ἁψαμένη βρόχον ἀ. μελάθρου{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.278.}}
**A.I.5** with the Article, where the sense of motion often disappears, οἱ ἀ. τῶν οἰκιῶν φεύγουσιν, i.e. οἱ ἐν ταῖς οἰκίαις φεύγουσιν ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.23}}; οἱ ἀ. τῶν πύργων.. ἐπαρήξουσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.4.18; αἴρειν τὰ ἀ. τῆς γῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 410b}}; αἱ ἵπποι αἱ ἀ. τοῦ ἅρματος variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8; ὁ Ἀθηναῖος ὁ ἀ. τοῦ στρατεύματος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.19; τὸν ἀ. γραμμᾶς κινεῖ λίθον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.18.}}
**A.I.6** partitive, λαχὼν ἀ. ληΐδος αἶσαν part **taken** **from** the booty, a share **of** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.40; αἴρεσθαι ἀ. τῶν καλπίδων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 539; ἀ. ἑκατὸν καὶ εἴκοσι παίδων εἷς μοῦνος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.27; ὀλίγοι ἀ. πολλῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.87, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1023.}}
**A.I.7** in Mathematics texts, of figures described **upon** a base, κῶνον ἀναγράφειν ἀ. κύκλου{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.19, etc.}}; τὸ ἀ. τῆς AB τετράγωνον the square **on** AB, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.47, compare 48}}; εἴδεα ἀ... {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἑλίκων” 10, 11.}}
**A.I.8** ἀ. ἀνθρώπου ἕως γυναικός man **and** woman, {{LXX|LXX.1Esd.9.40}}; ἀ. ἀρσενικοῦ ἕως θηλυκοῦ [prev. work] LXX.Num.5.3.
**A.I.9** **from being, instead of,** ἀθανάταν ἀ. θνατᾶς.. ἐποίησας Βερενίκαν{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.106.}}
**A.I.10** privative, **free from, without,** ἀ. πάσης ἀκαθαρσίας{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLips.” 16.19 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἀ. ζημίας{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PTeb” 420.4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**A.II** OF TIME, **from, after,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.54 ἀ. δείπνου θωρήσσοντο}} rising up **from,** i.e. **after,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133}}; ἀ. δείπνου εἶναι or γενέσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.126, 2.78, 5.18, +others; ἀ. τοῦ σιτίου πίνειν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὑγιεινῆς” 5; ἀ. τῶν σίτων διαπονεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.8}}; in narrative, τὸ ἀ. τούτου or το̄δε, **from** this point **onwards,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4, 2.99; ἀ. τούτου τοῦ χρόνου [prev. author] 1.82, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.5.8; τὸ ἀπ᾽ ἐκείνου 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 25; ἡμέρῃ δεκάτῃ ἀφ᾽ ἧς.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14, etc.; δευτέρῃ ἡμέρῃ ἀ. τῆς ἐμπρήσιος [prev. author] 8.55, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.18, etc.; ἀφ᾽ οὗ χρόνου [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.2.13}}; more often ἀπ᾽ or ἀφ᾽ οὗ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.44, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18, etc.; ἀφ᾽ οὗπερ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 177; ἀφ᾽ ἧς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 15}}; εὐθὺς ἀ. παλαιοῦ, ἀ. τοῦ πάνυ ἀρχαίου, **of** olden time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2, 2.15; ἀπ᾽ ἀρχᾶς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.25, etc.; ἀ. γενεᾶς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.8}}; ἀφ᾽ ἑσπέρας **from the beginning of** evening, i.e. **at** eventide, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.29}}; ἀ. πρώτου ὕπνου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 43; ἀ. μέσων νυκτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 218}}; ἀπ᾽ ἀγροῦ **fresh from** field-work, {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Mark.15.21, compare 7.4; ἀ. νουμηνίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.23}}; χρονίζειν ἀ. τοῦ καιροῦ tarry **beyond** the time, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.20.5}}; ἀ. τέλους ἐννέα μηνῶν **at** the end of..,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 24.8; γενόμενος ἀ. τῆς ἀρχῆς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 5: hence ἀ. ἀγωνοθετῶν an εχ-ἀγωνοθέτης, “IG” 3.398; ἀ. λογιστῶν “POxy.” 1103.3 (4th c.AD)}}; οἱ ἀ. ὑπατείας, = **consulares,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.1.9, etc.}}; but ἀ. τινος the **freedman of..,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(2).50.59(Tegea, 2nd c.AD), compare[prev. work]5(1).1391 (from Andania), 1473.}}
**A.III** OF ORIGIN, CAUSE, etc.:
**A.III.1** of that **from** which one is born, οὐ γὰρ ἀ. δρυός ἐσσι οὐδ᾽ ἀ. πέτρης not **sprung from** oak or rock, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.163; γίγνονται δ᾽ ἄρα ταί γ᾽ ἔκ τε κρηνέων ἀ. τ᾽ ἀλσέων 10.350, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 415, “OC” 571, etc.: sometimes ἀπό denotes remote, and ἐκ}} immediate, descent, τοὺς μὲν ἀ. θεῶν, τοὺς δ᾽ ἐξ αὐτῶν τῶν θεῶν γεγονότας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.81, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.150; πέμπτη ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ γέννα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 853}}; τρίτος ἀ. Διός third **in descent from** Zeus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 391c}}; οἱ ἀ. γένους τινός his descendants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 32; Περσέως ἀφ᾽ αἵματος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 509}}: of the place one **springs from,** ἵπποι.. ποταμοῦ ἄπο Σελλήεντος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.839. compare 849; Ἡρακλεῖδαι οἱ ἀ. Σπάρτης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.114, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.89, etc.; τοὺς ἀ. Φρυγίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.5, etc.:hence}}
**A.III.1.b** _metaphorically_ of things, Χαρίτων ἄπο κάλλος ἔχουσαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.18}}; θεῶν ἄπο μήδεα εἰδώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 12; γάλα ἀ. βοός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 611; μῆνις ἀφ᾽ ἡμῶν [prev. author] “Eu.” 314; ἡ ἀφ᾽ ὑμῶν τιμωρία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69}}; ὁ ἀ. τῶν πολεμίων φόβος fear **inspired by** the enemy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.53.}}
**A.III.1.c** of persons, οἱ ἀ. τῆς χώρας, τῆς πόλεως, country **folk,** towns**folk,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.6.8, 5.70.8}}; and so of connexion with the founder or leader of a sect, οἱ ἀ. Πυθαγόρου{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 14; οἱ ἀ. Πλάτωνος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 2}}; οἱ ἀ. τοῦ περιπάτου, ἀ. τῆς Στοᾶς, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 6; generally οἱ ἀ. φιλοσοφίας καὶ λόγων philosophers and learned men, [prev. passage]; οἱ ἀ. σκηνῆς καὶ θεάτρου}} stage players, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 2; οἱ ἀ. τῆς βουλῆς [prev. author] “Caes.” 10, etc.}}; ὁ ἀφ᾽ ἑστίας παῖς, see at {ἑστί; ἀπ᾽ ἐξωμίδος} **with only** an ἐξωμίς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.153.}}
**A.III.2** of the material **from** or **of** which a thing is made, εἵματα ἀ. ξύλου πεποιημένα{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.65; ἀπ᾽ ὄμφακος τεύχειν οἶνον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 970, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 704; ὅσσα ἀ. γλυκερῶ μέλιτος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.117; ἔνδυμα ἀ. τριχῶν καμήλου NT.Matt.3.4: hence στέφανος ἀ. ταλάντων ἑξήκοντα}} **of** or **weighing** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|60 talents, Canon Laws texts cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.92, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.1.7, “IG” 2.555.10, +others: hence of value, θύεν αἶγα ἀ. δραχμᾶν εἴκοσι “GDI” 3707 (from Cos)}}; κρᾶσις ἀ. τε τῆς ἡδονῆς συγκεκραμένη καὶ ἀ. τῆς λύπης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a}}; so, by an extension of this use, εἰδεχθής τις ἀ. τοῦ προσώπου ugly **of** countenance, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 28.4; θῆλυν ἀ. χροιῆς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.49; σεμνὸς ἀ. τοῦ σχήματος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 10.8.}}
**A.III.3** of the instrument **from** or **by** which a thing is done, τοὺς.. πέφνεν ἀπ᾽ ἀργυρέοιο βιοῖο by arrow **shot from** silver bow, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.605; τόξου ἄπο κρατεροῦ ὀλέκοντα φάλαγγας 8.279; ἐμῆς ἀπὸ χειρός 10.371, 11.675; so ἀ. χειρὸς ἐργάζεσθαι μεγάλα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 29}}; γυμνάζεσθαι ἀ. σκελῶν, χειρῶν, τραχήλου, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.9; μάχεσθαι ἀ. ἄκοντος 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.3.7; ἡ ἀ. τοῦ ξίφους μάχη 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.29; βάπτειν τὸν δάκτυλον ἀ. τοῦ αἵματος LXX.Lev.4.7.}}
**A.III.4** of the person **from** whom an act comes, i.e. **by** whom it is done, οὐδὲν μέγα ἔργον ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ ἐγένετο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14; ζήτησιν ἀ. σφέων γενέσθαι [prev. author] 2.54; ἐπράχθη οὐδὲν ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν ἔργον ἀξιόλογον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.17, compare 6.61; ἀ. τινος ὄνασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528a, etc.}}; so τἀπ᾽ ἐμοῦ, τἀπὸ σοῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 74, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1628; τὰ ἀ. τῶν Ἀθηναίων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.127}}; in later Greek frequently of the direct agent, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.34.8, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.4.12, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.12, NT.Luke.9.22, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 20.8.10, etc.}}; in codices this may sometimes be due to confusion with ὑπό, but{{Refs 1st c.AD+|compare “PMag.Par.” 1.256, “BGU” 1185.26(Aug.), “SIG” 820.8(Ephesus, 1st c.AD), etc.}}
**A.III.5** of the source **from** which life, power, etc., are sustained, ζῆν ἀπ᾽ ὕλης ἀγρίης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.203}}; ἀ. κτήνεων καὶ ἰχθύων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 216; ἀ. πολέμου [prev. author] 5.6; ἀπ᾽ ἐλαχίστων χρημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.14; ἀ. τῆς ἀγορᾶς [prev. author] “An.” 6.1.1; τρέφειν τὸ ναυτικὸν ἀ. τῶν νήσων [prev. author] “HG” 4.8.9, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99; ἀ. τῶν κοινῶν πλουτεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 569, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.124; ἀ. μικρῶν εὔνους.. γεγένησαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 788, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.102; ἀφ᾽ ὥρας ἐργάζεσθαι}}**quaestum corpore facere,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 14.}}
**A.III.6** of the cause, means, or occasion **from, by,** or **because of** which a thing is done, ἀ. τούτου κριοπρόσωπον τὤγαλμα τοῦ Διὸς ποιεῦσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.42}}; ἀ. τινος ἐπαινεῖσθαι, θαυμάζεσθαι, ὠφελεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.25, 6.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.2; ἀ. τῶν ξυμφορῶν διαβάλλεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.17; τὴν ἐπωνυμίαν ἔχειν ἀ. τινος [prev. author] 1.46; ἀ. λῃστείας τὸν βίον ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.9; ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν τῶν ἔργων κρίνειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.27}}; ἀ. τοῦ πάθους **in consequence of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.30; βλάπτειν τινὰ ἀ. τινος [prev. author] 7.29; κατασκευάσαντα τὸ πλοῖον ἀφ᾽ ὧν ὑπελάμβανε σωθήσεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.194}}; τρόπαιον ἀ. τινος εἱστήκει **on occasion of** his defeat, {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.320}}; τλήμων οὖσ᾽ ἀπ᾽ εὐτόλμου φρενός{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1302, compare 1643}}; ἀ. δικαιοσύνης **by reason of** it (variant for{ὑπό}), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.164}}; ἀ. τῶν αὐτῶν λημμάτων **on** the same **scale of** profits, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.34, etc.}}; for ὅσον ἀ. βοῆς ἕνεκα, see at {ἕνεκα}: hence in half adverbial usages, ἀ. σπουδῆς in earnest, eagerly, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.359}}; ἀ. τοῦἴσου, ἀ. τῆς ἴσης, or ἀπ᾽ ἴσης, equally, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99, 15, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.6, etc.; ἀπ᾽ ὀρθῆς καὶ δικαίας τῆς ψυχῆς [prev. author] 18.298; ἀ. ἀντιπάλου παρασκευῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91; ἀ. τοῦ προφανοῦς openly, [prev. work] 35}}; ἀ. τοῦ εὐθέος straightforwardly, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.43}}; ἀ. τοῦ αὐτομάτου **of** free-will, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 323c}}; ἀ. γλώσσης **by** word of mouth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123}} (but also, **from** hearsay, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 813); ἀ. στόματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142d}}; ἀπ᾽ ὄψεως **at** sight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.19}}; ἀ. χειρὸς λογίζεσθαι **on** your fingers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 656; πεύθομαι δ᾽ ἀπ᾽ ὀμμάτων νόστον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 988}}; ὀμμάτων ἄπο **in** the public gaze, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 216; ἀ. τοῦ κυάμου ἄρχοντας καθίστασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.9; ἡ βουλὴ ἡ ἀ. τοῦ κυάμου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66, compare “IG” 1.9; τοὺς ἀ. τοῦ κυάμου δισχιλίους ἄνδρας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 24.3}}; τριηράρχους αἱρεῖσθαι ἀ. τῆς οὐσίας Canon Laws texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.106}}; ἀφ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ **from** oneself, **on** one's own **account,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.6, etc.; ἀφ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ γνώμης [prev. author] 4.68}}; ἀ. συνθήματος, ἀ. παραγγέλματος, **by** agreement, **by** word of command, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.74, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.99}}; ἀ. σάλπιγγος **by sound of** trumpet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 3.12 (assuming variant)}}; ἐπίτροπος ἀ. τῶν λόγων, = Latin _procurator a rationibus, Ann.epigram._{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|1913.143a (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD).}}
**A.III.7** of the object spoken of, τὰ ἀ. τῆς νήσου οἰκότα ἐστί the things **told from** or **of** the island.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.195, compare 54, 7.195; νόμος κείμενος ἀ. τῶν τεχνῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 762.}}
**B** in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 5th c.AD: Cypria, ἀπύ takes _dative_, ἀπὺ τᾷ [ἁμέρᾳ] “IG” 5(2).6 (from Tegea)}}; ἀπὺ τᾷ ζᾷ{{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.8 H. (from Idalion).}}
**B.2** in later Greek ἀπό is found with _accusative_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.124.30 (4th-5th c.AD).}}
**C** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} frequent with Verbs in tmesi, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.214, etc., and sometimes in Prose, as 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.89.}}
**D** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words),
**D.1** **asunder,** as ἀποκόπτω, ἀπολύω, ἀποτέμνω: and hence, **away, off,** as ἀποβάλλω, ἀποβαίν; denoting, **remoual** of an accusation, as ἀπολογέομαι, ἀποψηφίζομαι.
**D.2** **finishing off, completing,** ἀπεργάζομαι, ἀπανδρόω, ἀπανθρωπίζω, ἀπογλαυκόω.
**D.3** **ceasing from, leaving off,** as ἀπαλγέω, ἀποκηδεύω, ἀπολοφύρομαι, ἀποζέω, ἀπανθίζω, ἀφυβρίζω.
**D.4** **back again,** as ἀποδίδωμι, ἀπολαμβάνω, ἀπόπλους: also, **in full,** or **what is one's own,** as ἀπέχω, ἀπολαμβάνω: frequently it only strengthens the sense of the simple.
**D.5** **by way of abuse,** as in ἀποκαλέω.
**D.6** almost ={ἀ-} _privative_; sometimes with Verbs, as ἀπαυδάω, ἀπαγορεύ; more frequently with Adjectives, as ἀποχρήματος, ἀπότιμος, ἀπόσιτος, ἀπόφονος.
**E** ἄπο, by anastrophe for ἀπό, when it follows its Noun, as ὀμμάτων ἄπο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1231, etc.}}; never in Prose.
**E.2** ἄπο for ἄπεστι, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 1.20, 5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 9.}}

576:
** ἀποβαίνω**,
_future_ -βήσομαι, with _Epic dialect_ _aorist 1_ **-εβήσετο**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.35:}}_aorist 2_ ἀπέβην: _perfect_ ἀποβέβηκα—in these tenses _intransitive_ (_present_ not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}:— **step off from** a place, νηὸς ἀ. **alight, disembark from** a ship, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.281}}; ἀπὸ τῶν νεῶν, ἀπὸ τῶν πλοίων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.86, 4.110; ἐκ τῶν νεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.12}}:_absolutely_, **disembark,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.111, etc.; ἀ. ἐς χώρην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8.β,compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 705, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.9, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.21; ἐς τὴν γῆν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.100}}; ἐξ ἵππων ἀ. ἐπὶ χθόνα **dismount** from a chariot, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.265, compare 11.619; ἵππων 17.480; but in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.23 τὸ ἀποβαίνειν}} seems to be the art of **leaping from** horse to horse (compare ἀποβάτης) τῇ συνωρίδι τοῦ ἀποβάντος{{Refs|“IG” 9(2).527.10 (from Larissa): generally, ἀβάτων ἀποβάς}} **having stepped off** ground on which none should step, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 167.}}
**2** **go away, depart,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.428, 5.133, etc.; ἀπέβη πρὸς μακρὸν Ολυμπον 24.468}}; πρὸς δώματα, κατὰ δῶμα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.657, 715; μετ᾽ ἀθανάτους 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.298: with _genitive_, ἀ. πεδίων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 140; ἀπὸ τῆς φάτνης 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.16}}; of death, ἀπὸ δὲ φθίμενοι βεβᾶσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1022}}; of hopes, **vanish, come to nought,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 909 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** of events, **issue, result from,** τὰ ἔμελλε ἀποβήσεσθαι ἀπὸ τῆς μάχης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.66; τἀναντία ἀπέβη}}**resulted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 39a, compare “Lg.” 782e; ὅ τι ἀποβήσεται [prev. author] “Prt.” 318a, etc.}}; τὸ ἀποβαῖνον, _contraction_ τὠποβαῖνον, **the issue, event,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.82, etc.}}; τὰ ἀποβαίνοντα, τὸ ἀποβάν, **the results,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.83, 2.87; τὰ ἀποβησόμενα}}**the probable results,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 3.38, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.103.}}
**II.2** frequently with an _adverb_ or other qualifying phrase, σκοπέειν.. τὴν τελευτὴν κῇ ἀποβήσεται how it will **turn out, issue,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; ἀ. τῇ περ εἶπε [prev. work] 86; ἀ. κατὰ τὸ ἐόν [prev. work] 97}}; ἀ. παρὰ δόξαν, ἀ. τοιοῦτον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.4, 7.23; τοιόνδ᾽ ἀπέβη τόδε πρᾶγμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1419, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.13; πολέμου τοιοῦτον ἀπέβη τὸ τέλος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 26.6.15; οὐδὲν αὐτῷ.. ὡς προσεδέχετο ἀπέβαινεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.104, compare 3.26; παρὰ γνώμην ἀ. 5.14; opposed to κατὰ γνώμαν ἀ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.38; πῶς ἡ φήμη δοκεῖ ὑμῖν ἀποβῆνα; 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.131.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **turn out well, succeed,** ἡ ὑπόσχεσις ἀπέβη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.39, compare 5.14}}; of dreams, **turn out true,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Div. Somn.” 463b10.}}
**II.4** of persons, with an _adjective_, **turn out, prove to be** so and so, ἀ. οὐ κοινοί **prove** partial, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53; ἀ. χείρους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 952b; φρενιτικοὶ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 405; τύραννος ἐκ βασιλέως ἀ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.13.7}}; also of a wound, ἰάσιμον ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 878c.}}
**II.4.b** with εἰς.., ἀ. εἰς τὰ πολιτικὰ οἱ τοιοῦτοι **prove fit** for public affairs, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 192a; ἐς ἀλαθινὸν ἄνδρ᾽ ἀ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.15.}}
**II.4.c** of conditions, etc., ἀπέβη ἐς μουναρχίην **things ended in** a monarchy, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82; εἰς ἓν τέλεον καὶ νεανικόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425c; ἀποβήσεται εἰς μαρτυρίαν NT.Luke.21.13.}}
**II.5** of space, μέγεθος μὲν ἦν πρὸς τὸν Ἠριδανὸν ἀποβεβηκῦα **reaching, extending to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 112a.}}
**II.6** τῷ ἀποβεβηκότι ποδί with the **hind** foot, opposed to τῷ προβεβηκότι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 706a9.}}
**B** causal, in _aorist 1_ ἀπέβησα, **cause to dismount, disembark, land** (in which sense ἀποβιβάζω serves as _present_), ἀ. στρατιήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.63, 6.107; ἐς τὴν Ψυττάλειαν [prev. author] 8.95.}}
**B.II** hence, in _passive_, τὸ ἀποβαινόμενον σκέλος a leg **put out so as not to bear the weight** of the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 52}}:—_active_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mochl.” 20.}}

577:
** ἀποβάλλω**,
_future_ -βαλῶ, **throw off,** ἀπὸ δὲ χλαῖναν βάλε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.183, compare 21.51; ἀπὸ φροντίδος ἄχθος.. βαλεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 166}}: with _genitive_, **throw off from,** ἀ. ὀμμάτων ὕπνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 692}}:—_middle_, **throw off from oneself, cast off,** δύναμιν βασιλέως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.29.}}
**2** **throw away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|hymnus ad Mercurium 388, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.40, etc.; ἀ. τὴν ἀσπίδα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 22, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.74, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.9, etc.}}; τὸν ἄνδρα τὸν πάρος ἀ. **reject** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 663, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 151c, etc.}}; ἀ. τὰ κέρατα, τὰς ὁπλάς, **cast, shed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 500a10, 604a15, +others}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 802b}}; οὐδεὶς ἀ. ἑκών **jettisons** cargo, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1110a9; ἀ. τὸν φιλέοντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.19}}; **expose** a child, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 4.9}}; **despise, reject,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 10}}:—_passive_, ἀποβληθεὶς τῆς τυραννίδος{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Dion.Brut.” 3.}}
**2.b** **throw away, sell too cheap,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.28.}}
**3** **lose,** τὴν τυραννίδα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60}}; τὰ πατρώια, τὸν στρατόν, τὴν κεφαλήν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.53, 8.65, +others; βιοτάν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 593; τὴν οὐσίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 811, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44e, etc.; ἱκανὸν μέρος τῶν ὄντων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.6, etc.}}; opposed to κτᾶσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.57, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291b41}}; ἀ.δόξαν, τέχνην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “de An.” 428b5, “Metaphorical” 1047a1, +others; ἀ. τι ὑπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.32; ἀ. πολλοὺς τῶν στρατιωτῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.7; τὸν εὐεργέταν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 878}}:—in _middle_, ἀγαθὸν πολίτην{{Refs|“SIG” 730.20 (from Olbia).}}
**3.b** Grammars, **drop** a letter, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 36.21, +others}}
**4** **degrade,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 630d.}}

578:
** ἀπο-βλέπω**,
_future_ **-βλέψομαι**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 12}} etc., but **-βλέψω** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 2.34}}: _perfect_ **-βέβλεφα**{{Refs 5th c.AD+|Antip.Stoic. 3.254 codices 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes}}:—_middle_, _present_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 2.47 (variant)}}: _aorist_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.247}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 726}}:—**look away from** all other objects at one, **gaze steadfastly,** ἐς ἐμέ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.135; εῐς σε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 246, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 162b, +others; ἐς ἀκτάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1206; ἐς μίαν τύχην [prev. author] “Hel.” 267; πρὸς τὸ Ηραιον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.61, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 431b; πρός τινα [prev. author] “Phd.” 115c, “Phdr.” 234d, +others}}
**2** **pay attention to, regard,** ἐς τὸ κακόν{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1171; πρὸς τὰ κοινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 422; εἰς τὰ πράγματα ἀ. φαύλως ἔχοντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.29; εἰς τὸ κέρδος μόνον 5th-6th c.BC: Demetrius Comicus Veteris Comoediae 4; εἰς τὴν μισθαποδοσίαν NT.Heb.11.26; ἐπί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 61d; κατάτι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 18.1; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 477c, +others; εἰς τὰ πράγματα καὶ πρὸς τοὺς λόγους ἀ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.1: with _accusative_, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Vertigine” 8, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 26, etc.}}
**3** of places, etc., **look, face** in a particular direction, πρὸς ὁδόν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 76.11 (of a statue); Ρήνου προχοάς “Anthologia Graeca” 9.283 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus); ἐπὶ τὴν ἀνατολήν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.5.5.}}
**4** **look upon** with love, wonder or admiration, **look at** as a model, with _accusative_, οὐ χρὴ.. μέγαν ὄλβον ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 593; ἀ. τινά 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 10, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.265; προϊόντα ἴσα θεῷ ἀπέβλεπον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.24}}; more frequently with a _preposition_, εἰς ἔμ᾽ Ελλὰς.. ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1378; ἡ σὴ πατρὶς εἰς σὲ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.8,compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58; εἰς τὴν εὐσέβειαν τῆς θεοῦ “SIG” 867.11 (from Ephesus)}}; so ἀ. πρός τινα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 928, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.30, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.2}}; of a vain person, ἀ. εἰς τὴν ἑαντῆς σκιάν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.22}}; of entire dependence, πάντα ἀ. εἰς τὸν ἐραστήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239b; εἰς ἀλλοτρίαν τράπεζαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.33}}; **look longingly,** ἐς τὸν ἀγρόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 32}}:—_passive_, **to be looked up to,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ec.” 726, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Socraticus Philosophus “fragment” 56D.; ὡς εὐδαίμων ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 13, compare “Somn.” 11.}}
**5** ἐς τοιόνδ᾽ ἀποβλέψας μόνον τροαῖον αὐτοῦ στήσομαι **with a single look,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 762.}}
**II** **look away,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 21.13: with _genitive_, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.1}}; ἀ. ἀπ᾽ ἀμφοτέρων **face** both ways, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.31}}; ἀπὸ τοῦ συμφέροντος Antip. [prev. cited]
**III** _middle_, **look at each other,** ταυρωπὸν ἀποβλεψάμενοι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.602.}}

579:
** ἀπό-βλητος**, ον η, ον
{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.127, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.19)}}, **to be thrown away** or **aside,** as worthless, οὔ τοι ἀπόβλητ᾽ ἐστὶ θεῶν ἐρικνδέα δῶρα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.65; οὔ τοι ἀπόβλητον ἔπος ἔσσεται 2.361; γίγαρτον 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 88, etc., compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 10 and late Prose, as 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.294, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 37, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.821a, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.31, 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 11.}}
**2** **capable of being thrown off,** Iamb.[same place]; **capable of being lost,** D.L.[same place]

580:
** ἀποβολ-ή**, ῆς, ἡ,
**throwing away,** e.g. ὅπλων [prev. work = 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges”] 943ef; **jettison,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.413}}; in Grammars, **dropping** of a letter, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.7, +others; τόνου “Synt.” 130.1.}}
**2** **loss,** opposed to κτῆσις, χρημάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 195e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1115a21, etc.; ἐπιστήμης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75d, compare 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 1.27: _plural_, τὰς τῶν κακῶν ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1362a36, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.32.}}

581:
Related to: ** ἀπογίγνομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ and later _Attic dialect_ **ἀπογίνομαι**, _future_ -γενήσομαι:— **to be away from, have no part in,** τῆς μάχης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.69; τῶν ἁμαρτημάτων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.39}}; **to be freed from,** κακῶν ἀπογεγονότες{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.2.2.}}
**II** _absolutely_, **to be taken away,** opposed to προσγίγνομαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Zeno Eleaticus Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 82b, “Lg.” 850a; ἀπεγίγνετο οὐδέν.. προσεγίγνετο δέ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.98}}: generally, **to be away, absent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.35: ἀπό τινος 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.126}}; of diseases, opposed to προσπίπτω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 1 (uncertain 1.).}}
**II.2** especially of death, ἀ. ἐκ τῶν οἰκίων **depart** from the house, **die** out of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.85}}; ἀπογενέσθαι alone, **to be dead,**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 136, compare “IG” 9(1).334.37 (_Locrian dialect_), 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 1.14; οἱ ἀπογενόμενοι}}**the dead,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34}}; ὁ ὕστατον αἰεὶ ἀ. he who **died** last, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.58. compare 5.4; οἱ ἀπογιγνόμενοι}}**the dying,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.111.}}
**II.3** **fall away, be lost,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.74}}; opposed to ἐκβλαστάνω, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.12.1.}}
**III** **arrive at,** ἀ. δωδεκαταῖος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.11.}}
**IV** **turn out, become,** τράχηλος σκληρὸς ἀ. {{Refs|[prev. work] 12 (uncertain reading)}}; νωθροὶ ἀ.{{Refs|[prev. author] “Prorrh.” 1.117 (uncertain 1.).}}
**V** ἀ. τὸ ἕκτον μέρος εἰς τρίχας καὶ αἷμα **goes into, is consumed in forming..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 595b1.}}

582:
** ἀπογρᾰφ-ή**, ἡ,
**register, list,** of lands or property, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 745d, 850d, etc.}}; of the πεντηκοστολόγοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.7; ἀ. τῆς οὐσίας “IG” 2.476.14}}; ἐφήβων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” (add.)1997c (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus)}}; **list** of moneys claimed by the state from private persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.4, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.32.}}
**2** **register of persons liable to taxation,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.2.2, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.1.1; ἡ κατ᾽ οἰκίαν ἀ. “PLond.” 2.260.79 (1st c.AD)}}, etc.; of the Roman **census-lists,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 16 (_plural_)}}; **muster-roll** of soldiers, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.23.9.}}
**3** generally, ἐξ ἀπογραφῆς λέγειν from **a written list,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Sotades Comicus 1.35.}}
**II** as _Attic dialect_ law-term, **copy of a declaration** made before a magistrate, **deposition** or **information** laid, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.3, 29.1, Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.51}}; ποιεῖσθαι ἀ., ={ἀπογράφειν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.1; τινὸς κατά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.23, cf. Harp.(which see)}}
**II.2** any **written declaration** before a magistrate, ἀ. ποιείσθωσαν δηλοῦντες κτλ.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 237 vii33 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; especially **declaration** of property or persons liable to taxation, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1147.26 (1st c.BC), etc.}}

583:
** ἀπογρᾰφ-ω**,
**write off, copy,** and in _middle_, **have** a thing **copied, have a copy made of,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 156a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.221b}}; **commit to writing,** ὀνόματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 113b.}}
**II** **enter in a list, register,** ἔθνος ἓν ἕκαστον ἀπέγραφον οἱ γραμματισταί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.100}}:—_passive_, **to be registered,** παρὰ τοῖς ἄρχουσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914c, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 272; πρὸς τὸν ἄρχοντα 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.44}}:—frequently _middle_, **register as one's own property,** ἄρνας δέκα δύο{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 246.10 (1st c.AD)}}; **declare** as liable to taxation, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTaur.” 1 vii 11 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**II.2** _middle_ also, **register, note for one's own use,** τὰ ἔτεα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.145, 3.136, compare 4th c.BC: Heraclides Ponticus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.554e, etc.}}
**II.3** _middle_, **register oneself,** οἱ Ἐλευσῖνάδε ἀπογραψάμενοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.9; πρὸς τὸν ταξίαρχον εἰς τὴν τάξιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.18; ἔξεστι πᾶσιν ἀπογραψαμένοις ἐκκλησιάζειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1297a24; φυλῆς ἧστινος ἂν ἀπογράψηται “IG” 2.54b11 (4th c.BC); ἀπεγράψανθο ἐμ πελτοφόρας [prev. work]7.2823 (_Boeotian dialect_); ἀ. εἰς ἀγῶνας πυγμὴν ἢ παγκροάτιον}}**enter oneself for..,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 39.1.8; but ἀπογραψάμενος πύκτης “Anthologia Graeca” 11.75 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus); γέρδιος -όμενος “POxy.” 252.4 (1st c.AD)}}; ἐπὶ στρατηγίαν ἀ. **enter as candidate for..,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 5; also ἀπογράψομαι ἐμαυτόν “PGrenf.” 1.45.6 (2nd c.BC)}}; αὑτοὺς ἀ.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 14.}}
**II.3.b** _metaphorically_, **subscribe to,** τῇ ἐμῇ αἱρέσει{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 271.18.}}
**III** as _Attic dialect_ law-term,
**III.1** ἀ. τινά **enter** a person's name for the purpose of accusing him, **give in a copy of the charge** against him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.12, etc.}}; generally, **inform against, denounce, X.HG**{{Refs|3.3.11: with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, ἀ. τινὰ μορίαν ἀφανίζειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.29}}: _middle_, **enter one's name as an accuser, indict,** τινά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.37: _absolutely_, [prev. passage]}}; of the magistrate who receives the charge, ἀπογράφεσθαι τὴν δίκην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.41:}} —in _passive_, of the person accused, ἀ. φόνου δίκην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]36, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.2, etc.}}
**III.2** **hand in a list** or **inventory** of property alleged to belong to the state, but held by a private person, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 17.4, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.1,2; ἀ. οὐσίαν τινὸς ὡς δημοσίαν οὖσαν 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 34}}; generally, **give in a list** or **statement** of property, τοῖς ἄρχουσι τὸ πλῆθος τῆς αὑτῶν οὐσίας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 754d; τὰ χωρία καὶ τὰς οἰκίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.54:}} —_passive_, {{Refs|40.22}}:—_middle_, **have such list given in, see it done,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.8, +others; ἀπογραφὴν ἀπογράψασθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.16; τίμημα μικρόν 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.39, compare 11.34}}; ἀ. ἀπόλειψιν **have** it **registered,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.17.}}
**III.2.b** with _accusative_ _person_, ἀπέγραψεν ταῦτα.. ἔχοντα αὑτόν **gave a written acknowledgement** that he was in possession of.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 27.14; but ἔχειν [prev. work]47:}} — in _passive_, **to be entered in the list** [of debts], {{Refs|[prev. author] 25.71.}}

584:
** ἀποδείκ-νῡμι**
(and ἀποδεικνύω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.20, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.14.3)}}, _Ionic dialect_ **ἀποδέκνῦμι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“GDI” 5653b14 (Chios, 5th c.BC)}}_future_ -δείξω, _Ionic dialect_ -δέξω:—
**A** **point away from** other objects **at** one, and so:
**A.I** **point out, display, make known,** whether by deed or word, σφι γνώμας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171, +others; τάφους καὶ συγγένειαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26; ἦθος τὸ πρόσθε τοκήων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 727; ἀρετήν 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Epitaphius” 29; τὰ τῆς τεχνης ἐξευρήματα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 9}}; **proclaim,** τὴν ἡμέρην “GDI” [same place];—_passive_, τῶν οῠρων ἀποδεχθέντων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 134b22 (Milet., 4th c.BC).}}
**A.I.2** **bring forward, produce,** μαρτύρια τούτων[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.45; πολλοὺς παῖδας [prev. author] 1.136, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1405, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.5; ἐπόχους 8.1.35; ἀ. τρόπαια 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.147; χρήματα πλεῖστ᾽ ἀ. ἐν τῷ κοινῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 774; μορφὴν ἑτέραν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 839.14 (variant{ἐπέδειξεν}): with}}]_participle_, ὑγιέα τινὰ ἐόντα ἀ. **produce** him safe and sound, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130, compare 134.}}
**A.I.3** **produce, deliver** accounts, τὸν λόγον{{Refs|[prev. author] 7.119; ἀ. τετρακόσια τάλαντα τετελεσμένα [prev. work] 118.}}
**A.I.4** **publish** a law, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.11.}}
**A.I.5** **appoint, assign,** τέμενος ἀ. τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.67, 89; βωμόν τινι [prev. author] 7.178; ἓν βουλευτήριον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15; γῆς ὅρους [prev. work] 72}}; τὴν τρίτην ἀ. ἐκκλησίαν **to fix, prescribe** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.25}}:—_passive_, τοῖσί ἐστι χῶρος ἀποδεδεγμένος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153; τροφὴ αὐτοῖσι τοιαύτη ἀποδέδεκται [prev. author] 2.65.}}
**A.I.5.b** with _infinitive_, κώμας ὅθεν ἀπέδειξαν οἱ ἡγεμόνες λαμβάνειν τὰ ἐπιτήδεια whence **they appointed** that they should receive.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.14:}} —_passive_, τοῖσι ἀποδεδέχθαι.. ἕλκειν (_impersonal_) **it had been appointed** them to draw, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.124.}}
**A.I.6** **show by argument, prove, demonstrate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1334, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.75b37, etc.; ἀ. σαφεῖς τὰς ἀποδείξεις 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.3; ἀ. ὡς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 548, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 472d}}; ὅτι.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 323c, etc.}}; πότερον.. ἢ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus1.114b}}: with _double_ _accusative_, **prove** one so and so, οὓς ἀποδείξω λέκτρων προδότας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 879, etc.; τοιούτους τινάς 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 4}}: followed by _participle_, ἀ. λόγῳ.. οὐδὲν μετεόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.94}}; ἀ. τινὰ λέγοντα οὐδέν **make it evident** that.., {{Refs|7.17, compare 2.133.}}
**A.II** **show forth** a person or thing **as** so and so, hence:
**A.II.1** **appoint, proclaim, create,** ἀ. τινὰ στρατηγόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.2, +others}}: with _infinitive_, στρατηγὸν εῖναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.25}}; ἀ. τούτους τὴν πόλιν νέμειν{{NT|[prev. work] 29; ἑαυτὸν ὅτι ἐστὶ θεός NT.2Thes.2.4}}:—_passive_, **to be so created,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.124, 162; μελεδωνοὶ ἀποδεδέχαται τῆς τροφῆς 2.65; ἀπεδέχθη εῖναι ἵππαρχος 7.154; αὐτοκράτωρ ἀποδέδεικται “POxy.” 1021.7 (1st c.AD)}}; ὕπατος ἀποδεδειγμένος, = Latin _consul designatus_, {{Refs|“OGI” 379.5 (from Tiflis), etc.}}
**A.II.2** **make, render,** mostly with an _adjective_, ἀ. τινὰς μοχθηροτάτους **make** them finished rascals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1011; ἀ. κρατίστους τοὺς λόχους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.23; γοργότερον ἀ. τὸν ἵππον [prev. author] “Eq.” 1.10; ζῷον ἀγριώτερον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516b}}: with a _substantive_, γέλωτα ἀ. τινά{{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 166a, compare “Phd.” 72c}}: with _participle_, βλέποντ᾽ ἀποδείξω σ᾽ ὀξύτερον τοῦ Λυγκέως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 210; ἀ. τινὰς ἀλλοτρίους ὄντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 179c:}} —_passive_, πολέμιοι ἀποδεδειγμένοι **declared** enemies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.26, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.200.}}
**A.II.3** **represent as,** ἀ. παῖδα πατρὸς ἑωυτῶν ἕκαστον ἐόντα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.143, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.17}}:— _passive_, ἀνδραγαθίη δ᾽ αὕτη ἀποδέδεκται **is represented, considered as..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.136}}; οὐδὲ.. οὗτοι ἐν τοῖσι ἄλλοισι θεοῖσι ἀποδεδέχαται **have** not **been considered, admitted** among.., {{Refs|2.43}}:—these two last examples may be _passive_ usages of ἀποδέχομαι.
**A.II.4** with _infinitive_, **ordain** a thing or person to be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.30, “Lac.” 10.7.}}
**A.II.5** **dedicate, consecrate,** θέατρον{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 29}}:—_passive_, νεὼς ἀποδέδεικται{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 5.}}
**B** _middle_, **show forth, exhibit** something of one's own, ἀποδέξασθαι τὴν γνώμην **deliver one's** opinion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.170, 207, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87; also ἀ. μεγάλα ἔργα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, +others; ἀξιαπηγητότατα [prev. work] 16; οὐδὲν λαμπρὸν ἔργον [prev. work] 174}}; ἀ. ἀρετάς **display** high qualities, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.49 (compare above 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2); πνεύματα εἰς ἄλληλα στάσιν.. ἀποδεικνύμενα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1088}}; of buildings and the like, μνημόσυνα ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.101; χώματα ἀξιοθέητα 1.184}}; οὐδεμίαν στρατηΐην ἀ. not **to have** any military service **to show,** {{Refs|2.111}}:—_passive_, ἔργα μεγάλα καὶ θαυμαστὰ.. ἀποδεχθέντα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prooem.”, compare}}
**B.2** _middle_ in _active_ sense, ἀποδεδειγμένοι ἦσαν ὅτι **had declared** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.9.}}
**C** _passive_, see above {{Refs|1.5, 11.1, 2, 3:}}_aorist_ ἀπεδείχθην is always _passive_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.154}}; and so mostly _perfect_ ἀποδέδειγμαι,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.136, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.26}}; but the _participle_ of the latter is sometimes _active_, see above {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 2.}}

585:
** ἀπόδειξις**,
_Ionic dialect_ -δεξις, εως, ἡ, (ἀποδείκνυμι) **showing forth, making** **known, exhibiting,** δι᾽ ἀπειροσύνην.. κοὐκ ἀπόδειξιν τῶν ὑπὸ γαίας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 196.}}
**2** **setting forth, publication,** Ἠροδότου.. ἱστορίης ἀπόδεξις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}**Prooem.;** ἀρχῆς ἀ. **an exposition, sketch** of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97; ἀ. περὶ τὸν πολιτικόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 277a; περί τινος “R.” 358b.}}
**3** **proof,** βουλομένοισί σφι γένοιτ᾽ ἂν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.101; ἀ. ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.19, etc.; especially by words, ἀποδείξεις εὑρίσκειν τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.3; ἀ. λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 162e; -ξεις φέρειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.5.5}}; χρῆσθαί τινι ἀποδείξει τινός use it **as a proof** of a thing, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.160a}}: in _plural_, **proofs,** or **arguments in proof** of, τινός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.300, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 73a; λέγειν τι ἐς ἀπόδειξιν τοῦ περιέσεσθαι τῷ πολέμῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13; ἄνευ ἀποδείξεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 92d; μετ᾽ ἀ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.1.3, +others}}; ἀ. λαμβάνειν.. τῶν μανθανόντων **test** them by examination, etc., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.736d; ἀ. ποιεῖσθαι τῶν ἐφήβων “IG” 2.470.40; ἀ. τέχνης}}**specimen,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 3.4; ἀ. αὑτοῖς δοῦναί τινος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.79f, etc.}}; **citation,** ποιητῶν καὶ ἱστοριαγράφων ἀποδείξεις{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 685.93 (Crete, 2nd c.BC).}}
**3.b** in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, **demonstration,** i. e. **deductive proof by syllogism,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 0.71b17, +others, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.25U., “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.89}}; opposed to inductive proof (ἐπαγωγή), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.81a40:—sometimes in a loose sense, ἀ. ῥητορικὴ ἐνθύμημα [prev. author] “Rh.” 1355a6.}}
**4** **appointment,** θεωρῶν{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 402.29 (Delph., 3rd c.BC).}}
**II** (from _middle_) ἀ. ἔργων μεγάλων **display, achievement** of mighty works, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207, compare 2.101, 148.}}

586:
** ἀποδεκᾰτόω**,
**tithe, take a tenth of,** τι {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ki.8.16; πάντα NT.Luke.18.12}}; ἀ. τὸν λαόν **take tithe of** them, {{LXX+NT|NT.Heb.7.5; δεκάτην ἀ. τινός LXX.Deu.14.22.}}
**II** **pay tithe of,** τι {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.28.22, NT.Matt.23.23, etc.}}

588:
** ἀποδέχομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἀποδέκομαι**, _future_ -δέξομαι: _aorist_ -εδεξάμην: _perfect_ -δέδεγμαι (for possible _passive_ usages of this tense see at {ἀποδείκνυμι} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.3)}}: —**accept,** καὶ οὐκ ἀπεδέξατ᾽ ἄποινα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.95, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 712, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.24, etc.}}; ἀ. γνώμην παρά τινος **accept** advice from him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.97; ἀπόδεξαί μου ὂ λέγω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 430d.}}
**2** **accept as a teacher, follow,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.1.1,etc.; ἀ. τινὰ σύμβουλον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 323c.}}
**3** **admit** to one's presence, τοὺς πρεσβευτάς{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.35.5; ἀ. αὐτὸν καὶ τὰ ῥηθέντα φιλοφρόνως 21.22.1, compare 3.66.8.}}
**4** mostly of **admitting into the mind,**
**4.a** **receive favourably, approve,** ἀπολογίαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.2}}; κατηγορίας, διαβολάς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3, 6.29}}; τοῖσι μὴ ἀποδεκομένοισι, with _accusative_ _infinitive_, those who do not **accept the story** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.43; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, δοῦναί τε καὶ ἀ. λόγον “R.” 531e; τὴν ἀπόκρισιν “Prt.” 329b; λόγον παρά τινος “Smp.” 194d, etc.; τι παρά τινος “Ti.” 29e; τί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.44, 7.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 54a, etc.: with _genitive_ _person_ mostly with}}_participle_ added, ἀ. τινὸς λέγοντος **receive** or **accept** a statement **from** him, i.e. **believe** or **agree with** his statements, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 92a, 92e; μὴ ἀποδέχεσθε τούτου φενακίζοντος ὑμᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.31; ἀ. μαθηματικοῦ πιθανολογοῦντος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094b26, compare “Rh.” 1395b8: without}}_participle_, οὐκ ἀποδέχομαι ἐμαυτοῦ ὡς τὸ ἓν δύο γέγονεν I cannot **satisfy** myself **in thinking** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 96e, compare “Euthphr.” 9e, “R.” 329e}}: _absolutely_, **to accept a statement, to be satisfied,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.277, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1263b16}}; ἀ. ἐάν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336d, 525d}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, **to be content with,** τῆς προαιρέσεως{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 24.2; τῶν εἰρημένων [prev. work] 59.9.}}
**4.b** generally, **approve, acknowledge,** τὴν τῶν ἐφήβων ἀρετήν{{Refs|“IG” 2.481.60, +others}}
**4.c** **take** or **understand** a thing, ὀρθῶς ἀ. τι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.15, compare “Cyr.” 8.7.10; ἱκανώτατα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 511d; τὰ τοιαῦτα δυσχερῶς πως ἀποδέχομαι [prev. author] “Euthphr.” 6a; ὑπόπτως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.53}}: with _genitive_ _person_ (the _accusative_ _of things_ being understood), οὕτως αὐτοῦ ἀποδεχώμεθα let us **understand** him thus (referring to what goes before), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 340c; μὴ χαλεπῶς ἀλλὰ πράως ἀποδεχώμεθα ἀλλήλων [prev. author] “Lg.” 634c.}}
**II** **receive back, recover,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.33}}; opposed to ἀποδιδόναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26.}}
**III** **sustain, hold out against,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.43.3, 5.51.1.}}

589:
** ἀποδημ-έω**,
_Doric dialect_ **ἀπο-δᾱμέω**, _perfect_ ἀπεδήμηκα uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 66}}:— **to be away from home, be abroad** or **on one's travels,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29, 4.1, 152, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 371, etc.}}; of foreign service, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lys.” 101}}; opposed to ἐπιδημεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.69}}: _metaphorically_, **to be absent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.37; ὁ νοῦς παρὼν ἀποδημεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1120: sometimes with _genitive_, ἀποδημεῖν οἰκίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954b; ἀπὸ τῆς ἑωυτῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.117; ἐκ τῆς πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 53a; οὐκ ἔξεστι ἀποδημεῖν τοῖς Λακεδαιμονίοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 543.}}
**2** **go abroad,** παρά τινα to visit him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.124}}; ἀ. ἐς Αἴγιναν κατὰ τοὺς Αἰακίδας **go abroad** to Aegina to fetch the Aeacidae, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.84; ἀ. ἐπὶ δεῖπνον εἰς Θετταλίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 53e; ἐνθένδε εἰς ἄλλον τόπον [prev. author] “Ap.” 40e; κατ᾽ ἐμπορίαν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 21, 57 (variant{ἐπί}); πρὸς τὰ ἱερά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.7.3; ποῖ γῆς ἀπεδήμει; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 48; ἄλλοσε ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 579b; ἐκεῖσε [prev. author] “Phd.” 61e.}}]

591:
** ἀποδίδωμι**,
_future_ -δώσω: _aorist 1_ ἀπέδωκα: _aorist 2_ ἀπέδων{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 276.9, shortened}}_infinitive_ ἀποδοῦν probably in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—**give up** or **back, restore, return,** τινί τι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}: especially **render what is due, pay,** as debts, penalties, submission, honour, etc., τοκεῦσι θρέπτρα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.478}}; ἀ. τινὶ λώβην **give** him **back** his insuit, i.e. **make atonement for** it, {{Refs|[prev. work] 9.387 (tmesis)}}; τὴν πλημμέλειαν{{LXX+6th c.BC+|LXX.Num.5.7; εὖ ἔρδοντι κακὴν ἀ. ἀμοιβήν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1263; ἀ. τὴν ὁμοίην τινί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.119; ἀμοιβάς 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 92; κακὸν ἀντ᾽ ἀγαθοῦ [prev. author] 93}}; ἀ. τὸ μόρσιμον **pay the debt of** fate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.44; τὸ χρέος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.136; τὸν ναῦλον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 270}}; τὴν ζημίαν, τὴν καταδίκην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70, 5.50; τὴν φερνήν “PEleph.” 1.11 (4th c.BC)}}; εὐχάς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.10; ἀ. ὀπίσω ἐς Ἡρακλείδας τὴν ἀρχήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.13, etc.; πόλεις ἀ. τοῖς παρακαταθεμένοις 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.85; ἀ. χάριτας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.24; οὐκ ἐς χάριν ἀλλ᾽ ἐς ὀφείλημα τὴν ἀρετὴν ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40; ἀ. χάριν τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.73; [τὴν πόλιν] ἀ. τοῖς ἐπιγιγνομένοις οἵανπερ παρὰ τῶν πατέρων παρελάβομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.30}}:—_passive_, ἔως κ᾽ ἀπὸ πάντα δοθείη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.78}}; ἀ. μισθός, χάριτες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1066, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.63.}}
**2** **assign,** ταῖς γυναιξὶ μουσικὴν καὶ γυμναστικήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 456b; τὸ δίκαιον καὶ τὸ συμφέρον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354b3, compare 1356a15; τὸ πρὸς ἀλκὴν ὅπλον ἀ. ἡφύσις [prev. author] “GA” 759b3, etc.}}
**2.b** **refer** to one, as belonging to his department, εἰς τοὺς κριτὰς τὴν κρίσιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 765b}}; ἀ. εἰς τὴν βουλὴν περὶ αὐτῶν **refer** their case to the Council, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.6, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.2, etc.}}
**3** **render, yield,** of land, ἐπὶ διηκόσια ἀποδοῦναι (i.e. καρπόν) **yield fruit** two hundred-fold, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193; τἅλλα δ᾽ ἅν τις καταβάλη ἀπέδωκεν ὀρθεῶς 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 38}}; ἤν ἡ χώρη κατὰ λόγον ἐπιδιδοῖ ἐς ὕψος καὶ τὸ ὅμοιον ἀποδιδοῖ ἐς αὔξησιν **renders, makes** a like increase in extent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13:—hence perhaps _metaphorically_, τὸ ἔργον ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1106a16; ἀ. δάκρυ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 489.}}
**4** **concede, allow,** with _infinitive_, **suffer** or **allow** a person to do, ἀ. τισὶ αὐτονομεῖσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.144, compare 3.36; εἰ δὲ τοῖς μὲν.. ἐπιτάττειν ἀποδώσετε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.30; ἀ. κολάζειν [prev. author] 23.56; τῷ δικαστηρίῳ ἀποδίδοται τοῦ φόνου τὰς δίκας δικάζειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.30; ἀ. τινὶ ζητεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1341b30, compare “Po.” 1454b5; also οὔτε ἀπολογίας ἀποδοθείσης 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.3}}; ἐπειδὰν αὐτοῖς ὁ λόγος ἀποδοθῆ when right of speech is **allowed** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.54.}}
**5** ἀ. τινά with an _adjective_, **render** or **make** so and so, like{ἀποδείκνυμι}, ἀ. τὴν τέρψιν βεβαιοτέραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.46; τέλειον ἀ. τὸ τέκνον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 733b1; δεῖ τὰς ἐνεργείας ποιὰς ἀ. [prev. author] “EN” 1103b22; μετριωτέραν τὴν ὑπερηφανίαν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.16.}}
**5.b** **exhibit, display,** τὴν ὑπάρχουσαν ἀρετήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.109}}; ἀ. τὴν ἰδίαν μορφήν **render, express** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1454b10}}; ἀ. φαντασίαν τινός **present** appearance of, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.71 W., +others}}
**6** **deliver over, give up,** e.g. as a slave, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 239; ἀ. τὸν μιαρὸν τῶ χρόνῳ φῆναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.4.11.}}
**7** ἀ. ἐπιστολήν **deliver** a letter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.10, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 745.}}
**8** ἀ. τὸν ἀγῶνα ὀρθῶς καὶ καλῶς **bring** it **to a conclusion,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 149.}}
**9** λόγον ἀ. **render** an account, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.48:}} —_passive_, μαρτυρίαι ἀ. Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.137.}}
**10** ἀ. ὅρκον, see at {ὅρκος}.
**11** **give an account** or **definition** of a thing, **explain** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 150; ἀ. τί ἐστί τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 2b8, compare 1a10, “Metaphorical” 1040b30, +others; ἑπομένως τούτοις ἀ. τὴν ψυχήν [prev. author]}}**de.An.**{{Refs|405a4, compare “Ph.” 194b34, +others}}; also, **use by way of definition,** ὁ μὲν τὴν ὕλην ἀποδίδωσιν, ὁ δὲ τὸ εἶδος{{Refs|[prev. author] “de An.” 403b1}}; simply, **define,** τὸν ἄνθρωπον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.272}}; **expound,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.14, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.3, 119G., 143 G.}}; **render, interpret** one word **by** another, ἀ. τὴν κοτύλην ἄλεισον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.479c}}; **explain, interpret,** τὸ φωνὴν αἵματος βοᾶν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.209}}:—_passive_, βέλτιον ἀποδοθήσεται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1 P. 15 U.; ἀκριβεστέρως ἀποδοθήσεται 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 45.21; ἀ. τι πρός τι}}**use with reference to,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 281.10, cf. Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 538.}}
**12** **attach** or **append, make dependent upon,** τί τινι or εἴς τι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 24.5, 6, 2.}}
**13** ἀ. τί τινος **assign** a property **to** a thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 128b28.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **return, recur,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 722a8, “HA” 585b32.}}
**II.2** Rhet. and Grammars, **introduce a clause answering to the** πρότασις, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1407a20; διὰ μακροῦ ἀ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 9, etc.; compare ἀπόδοσις 11.2}}; οὐκ ἀποδίδωσι τὸ ἐπεί **has** no **apodosis,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.103}}; especially in similes, **complete the comparison,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1413a11.}}
**II.3** in Tactics, **turn** **back to face the enemy,** εἰς ὀρθόν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 10.12, etc.}}
**II.4** in Medicine texts in _passive_, **to be evacuated,** σὺν τοῖς περιττώμασιν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.82.}}
**III** _middle_, **give away of one's own will, sell,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 585, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.70, etc.}}; ἀ. τι ἐς τὴν Ἑλλάδα **take** to Greece **and sell** it there, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.56: with _genitive_ pretii, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 830, “Pax” 1237; οὐκ ἄν ἀπεδόμην πολλοῦ τὰς ἐλπίδας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98b}}; ἀ. τῆς ἀξίας, τοῦ εὑρίσκοντος, **sell for** its worth, **for** what it will fetch, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.96}}; ὅταν τις οἰκέτην πονηρὸν πωλῆ (= offer for sale) καὶ ἀποδῶται τοῦ εὑρόντος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.5.5, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 15.4; διδοῦσι [τὰς νέας] πενταδράχμους ἀποδόμενοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.89}}; ἀ. εἰσαγγελίαν **sell,** i.e. **take a bribe to forgo,** the information, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.47; οἱ δραχμῆς ἄν ἀποδόμενοι τὴν πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.48}}; at Athens, especially **farm out** the public taxes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.60, opposed to ὠνέομαι}}: _metaphorically_, οἷον πρὸς ἄργυρον τὴν δόξαν τὰς ψυχάς{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.42b}}:—_active_ and _middle_ are distinguished in Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.97 πάντα ἀποδόμενος τὰ ἡμίσεα ἀποδώσω τῷ ἀποκτείναντι}}: but _active_ is used in _middle_ sense in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.62 (assuming variant), compare “Foed.Delph.Pell.” 2 A 22, and possibly in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 239, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1235}}: _middle_ for _active_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator “fragment” 54}}:—_passive_, **to be sold,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

593:
** ἀποδοκῐμ-άζω**,
**reject on scrutiny** or **trial, reject** a candidate **from want of qualification,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.130, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 14}}:—_passive_, λαχὼν ἀπεδοκιμάσθη ἄρχειν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.10, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.30.}}
**2** generally, **reject as unworthy** or **unfit,** πασσόφους ἄνδρας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 181b; ἵππον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.13; νόμους [prev. author] “Mem.” 4.4.14; ἀργύριον 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.11; τὴν [τοῦ αὐλοῦ] χρῆσιν ἐκ τῶν νέων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1341a26, compare 37 (_passive_)}}; [ἡ ὄρνις] ἀ. τὰ αὑτῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HA” 618a17; τὴν τοιαύτην διατριβήν 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 8.12; τὸ ποιεῖν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.47}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 584.26.}}
**II** **conclude, judge,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 117.}}

594:
** ἀποδοχ-ή**,
_Aeolic dialect_ ἀπυδοχά, ἡ, **receiving back, having restored to one,** opposed to ἀπόδοσις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.81.}}
**2** **entertainment, reception,** ξένων{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.2.12 (assuming variant).}}
**3** **place of reception,** γῆ ἀ. πάντων{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 15.}}
**II** **acceptance, approbation, favour,** ἀποδοχῆς τυγχάνειν παρά τινι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.5.5, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.14.4; ἀ. ἀξιοῦσθαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.56.1, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.53; μετ᾽ εὐχαριστίας καὶ ἀποδοχῆς 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.2; εἶναι ἐν ἀ. τῷ δήμῳ “SIG” 807.21 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 1st c.AD)}}; εἶναι ἐν τῇ καλλίστῃ ἀ.{{Refs|“AJA” 18.324 (from Sardes)}}; ἐν ἀ. ἔχην τινά{{Refs|“GDI” 311 (from Cyme)}}; πάσης ἀ. ἄξιος{{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.1Tim.1.15, compare “SIG” 867.21 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 20.}}
**III** **acceptation, meaning** of terms, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.232.}}

595:
** ἀπόθεσις**, εως, ἡ,
(ἀποτίθημι) **laying up in store,** σκευῶν{{Refs|“SIG” 1106.107 (from Cos)}}; εἰς ἀ. γενέσθαι to be **stored up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844d; γάλα χρήσιμον εἰς ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 522a26}}; ἡ ἀ. τῆς τροφῆς, of bees,{{Refs|622b26; τὴν ἀ. τῆς θήρας ποιεῖσθαι 623a12}}; **preserving,** of fish, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.23.}}
**2** **the final movement in setting** a dislocated or fractured limb, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).332, +others, 6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus}}**in Hp.Fract.**{{Refs|12.273}} C.; _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 19.}}
**3** κατ᾽ ἀπόθεσιν, of **internal** abscesses, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).103.}}
**II** **putting aside, making away with, getting rid of,** ῥύπου{{NT|NT.1Pet.3.21, compare 2.1.14.}}
**II.2** **exposure** of children, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335b19; compare ἀποτίθημι 11.7.}}
**II.3** **resignation** of an office, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.3, compare “SIG” 900.16(4th c.AD).}}
**II.4** ἀ. κώλου, περιόδου, **close** or **cadence** of a phrase, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 19, cf. Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 176}}; so in metres, ={κατάληξις}, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 4 at Title Page}}
**III** ={ἀποδυτήριον}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hipp.” 5.}}

596:
** ἀπο-θήκη**, ἡ,
**any place wherein to lay up** a thing, **magazine, storehouse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.97; ἀ. βιβλίων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 5; ἀ. σωμάτων}}**burial-place,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cont.” 22.}}
**2** **refuge,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philistus Historicus 59.}}
**II** **anything laid by, store,** ἀποθήκην ποιεῖσθαι ἐς τὸν Πέρσην lay up **store** of favour with him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.109.}}

598:
** ἀπο-θλίβω** [ῑ],
**squeeze out,** τοὺς ὄρχεις{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 632a17; ὑπόστασιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 29; τὸν ἐκ τοῦ βότρυος ἀποθλιβόμενον οἶνον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.62}}; τῆς χώρας **from** the place, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Jud.Voc.” 2.}}
**2** **press** or **force back,** τὸ αἷμα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 587a22:—in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 237 Ruhnken restored ἀπολέψειν.}}
**3** **press tightly,** τὰ κράσπεδα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.30; ἀ. τινά}}**press upon, crowd,** {{NT|NT.Luke.8.45.}}
**4** **crush,** {{LXX|LXX.Num.22.25.}}
**II** Grammars, **drop** a letter in the middle of a word, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 185.3 (_passive_).}}
**III** **oppress much,** {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Exo.3.9, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Judg.10.12:—}}_passive_, πρὸς τὸ τὴν πόλιν ἀποθλιβῆναι Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 11 A 9 (2nd c.BC).}}

599:
** ἀποθνήσκω**,
_future_ -θᾰνοῦμαι, _Ionic dialect_ -θανέομαι or **-εῦμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.143, 7.134:}} —strengthened for θνῄσκω, **die,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.27, and once in Trag. (5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578.6)}}; in Comedy texts and Prose the usual form of the _present_; σεῦ ἀποτεθνηῶτος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.432; ἀποθνῄσκων περὶ φασγάνῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.424; βόες δ᾽ ἀποτέθνασαν ἤδη 12.393; ἐκ τῶν τρωμάτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.63; ὑπὸ λιμοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126: with _dative_, βρόμῳ κεραυνοῦ Pi.[same place]}}; νόσῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.84: with _accusative_ _cognate_, θάνατον ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.3, etc.; εἰς ἕτερον ζῆν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365d}}; **to be ready to die,** of laughter, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 15; ἀ. τῷ δέει 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1191a35.}}
**II** serving as _passive_ of ἀποκτείνω, **to be put to death, slain,** ὑπό τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.137, 7.154}}; especially by judicial sentence, ἀποθανεῖν ὑπὸ τῆς πόλεως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 93, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29d, 32d, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1397a30 (variant).}}
**III** **renounce,** νόμῳ{{NT|NT.Gal.2.19; ἀπό τινος NT.Col.2.20.}}
